00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.160
of discussions around
what is really important

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.580
to consider when you're starting
this journey into the cloud.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.320
Because this is
definitely a journey.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.440
So I want to spend a few
minutes on a few things.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.040
First of all, for a lot of
you, it's a bit of a discovery.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.600
The question mark is, what is
this Google Cloud business?

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.690
For others, you're
going to deep-dive

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.690
into some part of the story and
some part of the technology.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.110
So why don't we start, first
of all, about Google Cloud?

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:17.500
If you think about Google
Cloud, Google Cloud

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.390
is pretty much the
extension of everything

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.540
we have been doing at Google
since we created the company.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:23.980
I was not there in 1998.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.690
I had an immense
privilege, actually,

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.610
to start this
business in Europe,

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.260
the Google Cloud business,
six, seven years ago.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:35.040
But when you think about 1998,
when Larry and Sergey created

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.750
the company, they had
a vision and a mission,

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.700
which still is very
much in play today.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.700
It's really to organize
the world information

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:48.130
and to make it accessible and
useful for everyone on Earth.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.500
And think about the
enterprise space.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.320
We are running, we are
part of great companies,

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.390
whether you are digital
natives or you're

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.610
an enterprise company.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.020
And it's exactly the same.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:04:59.830
Think about it.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.110
It's really about the data.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:06.590
We are just managing,
swimming into the data,

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:10.600
whether it's customer data
or it's industrial data.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.760
And what we really
care at Google

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:17.920
is really to turn this data
into a comprehensive business

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.830
models, into innovation,
so that you can not only

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:22.700
change the way you
run your business,

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:27.880
but, more importantly, really
build a competitive advantage

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.890
to build what's next.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:32.470
And this is why this
event is called Next.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:34.510
We really care
about what is going

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:36.970
to be next, which
products, services

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.650
you are going to create
for your customers,

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.672
and, again, to be
more competitive.

00:05:41.672 --> 00:05:43.630
And at the end of the
day, actually, at Google,

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:46.600
we realized that it was
just pretty much what

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.690
we were doing, as I said, since
the beginning of our story.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.810
But this is the way we
have built our own company.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:55.840
And this is the way we
have grown our company.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.190
If you think about
it, Google Cloud

00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:59.770
is really enterprise business.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:04.270
And it's really built on three
or four very important pillars.

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:07.180
One is the data center
and the infrastructure

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:08.110
we have been building.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:10.829
The other one is
the amazing network

00:06:10.829 --> 00:06:12.370
that we have built,
around the globe,

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:16.450
actually, to be able
to transfer this data

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.200
and to process this data.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:19.990
The other one is
mobility if you had

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:23.810
to think about a third pillar.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:27.070
And the last one is the one
we are talking about pretty

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:29.530
much everywhere, which
is machine learning

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:31.480
and artificial
intelligence, which

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.790
is being baked in everything
we are doing right now.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:36.700
So think about the platform.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.980
At the end of the
day, our mission

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:45.700
is really to deliver a highly
scalable, open, and secure

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:48.130
platform, to build your
business, if you're

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:50.460
digital natives,
or to build what's

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:52.960
next if you are an
enterprise company.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.430
And we've got a few layers.

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:55.510
And this is very
important, actually,

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.093
because this is
really how we're going

00:06:57.093 --> 00:06:58.600
to run this day, together.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:02.630
The first one, which is super
important is infrastructure.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.792
I mean nothing would
have been happen

00:07:04.792 --> 00:07:07.000
without the infrastructure
that we have been building

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.640
at Google, all
these data centers

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.790
that we have been building,
that we are expanding across all

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:14.030
the different geographies.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.240
And a good thing, if you
think about the Netherlands

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.080
and the Benelux, we have one
of the largest infrastructure

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:21.550
and data center,
around the world,

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:24.350
in this region, which
is really great.

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.310
This is infrastructure
that we have inherited.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.500
And this infrastructure
that is hosting--

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.570
probably you've
heard about that--

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:34.390
more than seven services
that are being used

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.740
by more than a billion users.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:37.940
And that's super important.

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:39.520
I mean there is no
way you're going

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.380
to be able to manage,
in your enterprise,

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.010
the volume of data that
is, right now, floating

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:50.430
into your company without
the super capacity, actually,

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:51.550
to host this data.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.460
So this is also--

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:56.070
and that, you probably
don't know, actually--

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.810
if you think about our
Google Cloud customer,

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.960
I mean they are, themselves,
servicing more than a billion

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:04.270
users within their community.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:06.100
This is really
the infrastructure

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:10.140
that is hosting our amazing
products around data analytics

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:11.355
and around machine learning.

00:08:11.355 --> 00:08:12.230
So this is important.

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:14.200
The other thing which
is very important

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:17.210
is what we are providing
to the developer community.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:20.930
And a lot of things are going
to be demonstrated today.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:25.600
Whether its all the smarter
APIs that you can find,

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:29.530
the translation API, the
Video APIs, the Vision API,

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:32.110
all these sets of APIs
run natural language.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:35.320
They are, here, totally
open and usable.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:39.010
Or these amazing,
actually, products

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:42.220
around [INAUDIBLE] management
or Kubernetes and, again,

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:43.520
all the smart APIs.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.269
So we are really investing for
the developer community, which

00:08:47.269 --> 00:08:49.060
is extremely important,
actually, to build,

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.180
again, your next applications.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.930
The other thing that
we care a lot about--

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:54.970
and I know that we've
got a lot of companies

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:59.450
in the room that are using these
products-- is productivity.

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:03.250
So we not only want to
deliver the right technology

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:06.250
to build your next application,
but we really want, actually,

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:09.960
to help you change the way you
are working within your company

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:13.900
by better engaging
your employees

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:16.900
through some live
collaboration processes.

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:19.220
I had a chance to, again,
join this company six,

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:20.350
seven years ago.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:22.570
And I don't remember
the last time I really

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:24.340
attached a file in an email.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:25.630
This is totally over.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:27.920
I mean it's really about
live collaboration.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.590
It's about engaging
people, together.

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.360
And we will have
a specific session

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:34.090
on that, which is totally
changing, actually, the way

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:38.110
people are working and really
implementing a new culture

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:39.680
within your company.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:41.920
The other thing we
really care a lot about,

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.250
and that you probably heard,
and you will hear more and more,

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.830
is all about our
mobile strategy.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:49.700
And whether it's
Android, of course,

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:53.590
which is by far the most
used operating system

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:56.380
in the environment, in
the mobile environment--

00:09:56.380 --> 00:09:58.690
now we're getting into
the enterprise space--

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:02.810
or the Chrome OS,
which is probably

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.950
the more secure and
sharable and easy

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:10.270
operating system to access
all of this information.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:11.860
There are a lot of
studies, right now,

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:16.960
stating that, by 2018, 25%
of the Fortune 500 companies

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:20.110
will start using Chrome
OS, as an operating system,

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:22.630
to securely work
into their company

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:24.830
and to their environments.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:28.540
So this is the platform,
this open platform

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:31.790
that we are, right now,
delivering to the marketplace.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:34.000
So what happens in 2017?

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.300
I mean we all know that the
cloud is the thing that we

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.370
need to embrace and to adopt.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.550
We all know that every single
business, every single industry

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.020
is being disrupted.

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:47.110
I have, again, the privilege
to meet a lot of executives.

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:49.210
I used to live in
America, where we

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:52.180
were hosting a lot of executive
in our existing briefing

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:53.200
centers.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.510
And every single
executive, coming

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:58.510
from any kind of
industries, are telling us

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:00.460
that their industry
is being disrupted.

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:02.950
And they really want
to partner with us

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:05.740
to figure out how
they can be leading

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:11.360
this next generation and their
business within their industry.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:15.280
So at the same time, the market
is moving extremely fast,

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:17.860
we are also investing
a lot in this business.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:20.950
And to be very candid,
actually, two, three,

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:23.890
four years ago, we were being
questioned about how serious

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:25.810
Google was with this business.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.187
And for the past
24 to 36 months--

00:11:28.187 --> 00:11:29.770
and I hope you've
seen that, actually,

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:31.645
and we would like to
get your feedback, also.

00:11:31.645 --> 00:11:33.400
It's really important
to get that today--

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:37.060
we have been investing in
making this company really

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:42.610
an enterprise-ready company,
by our engineers, actually,

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:45.910
releasing 500 releases
during the next few years,

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:49.110
or by launching these
new great products--

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:51.590
and we have dedicated
sessions to discover products,

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:54.610
like a cloud machine
learning or like Spanner--

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:57.740
but also by implementing
some technical resources

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:00.780
and connecting, again, our
engineers to our company.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:01.970
So this is super important.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.280
There is another thing which
is extremely important.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.020
And you will have
some sessions today.

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:09.170
Its compliance,
policy, and security.

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:10.850
At the same time,
everybody is moving

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:13.310
to the cloud, what we
are seeing is the highly

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:15.320
regulated industries
and companies are

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.520
going to the cloud.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.770
And especially in Europe
and in this country,

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.020
in particular, it is
super important, actually,

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:25.850
to spend a lot of time, efforts
on compliance and security.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.520
The other thing that we are
investing a lot of time,

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:31.400
at Google Cloud, is to
making sure that not only

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:35.530
we can provide the best tools
and processes around machine

00:12:35.530 --> 00:12:38.030
learning, which I think is
definitely where we are leading

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:41.330
the game, or analytics or
big data and big query,

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:44.750
but its really about
lifting and shifting

00:12:44.750 --> 00:12:47.780
your on-premise
workloads into the cloud.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.670
And we have dedicated
teams, actually,

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:51.120
to help you migrating this.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:55.910
So we are here to solve business
issues, whichever, actually,

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:57.800
your are belonging to.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:01.760
So whether you're a
hospital or a bank,

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.340
or whether you are
running a store

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:08.450
or you are building a video
game or you're running a media

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:12.690
company or you're making energy,
I think we are seeing a lot,

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:16.030
a lot of companies coming to
us and adopting our business.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:19.040
So for instance, we've got
a lot of digital natives,

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:20.720
historically,
companies in Europe

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:23.570
very close to my heart,
actually, like Spotify

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:26.660
or very famous companies
like eBay or Snapchat.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:30.290
They literally built
Snapchat, built its business

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:31.940
on Google Cloud.

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:33.880
Companies like Spotify,
that I mentioned,

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.320
moved all their workloads
into Google Cloud,

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:38.750
actually, to go to the
next step and really

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:40.970
provide a much better
business experience

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:44.660
and build, actually, competitive
advantage to be leading,

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.970
actually, the market
they're operating into.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:48.530
But you're seeing,
on these styles,

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:50.570
a lot of very
traditional companies.

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:52.850
And this is where it's
fascinating, actually,

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.720
to be part of their journey,
on how we can really

00:13:55.720 --> 00:14:00.500
coach these companies to move,
successfully, to the cloud

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:01.940
and step by step.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:05.624
I think one of the
largest company

00:14:05.624 --> 00:14:07.040
we are starting
the journey with--

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:09.080
and probably you had
a chance to listen

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:13.430
to Darryl West, the global CIO,
when he was in San Francisco--

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:14.840
is HSBC.

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:16.220
Think about HSBC.

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:19.310
It's probably the oldest
bank in the world.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:22.160
They have been in the
business for 150 years.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.030
And it's probably one of the
most conservative companies

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:29.210
in the world, I mean, in
the most regulated industry,

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:31.520
of course, which is
the banking industry.

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:33.650
And it's fascinating to
see that a company that

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:36.560
has been created 150
years ago, of course,

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:39.140
has generated a
lot, a lot of data.

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:42.830
Actually, it's funny, because
it took them 130 years

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:46.400
to start measuring their
data into terabytes.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:48.680
And for the past 20
years, actually, they

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:51.350
have generated 15 petabytes.

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:53.000
And now, they are
thinking about how

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.790
they are going to handle an
additional 5 petabytes just

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:57.794
for the next 12 months.

00:14:57.794 --> 00:14:59.210
This is what we
are talking about.

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.010
It's really a total explosion.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:03.300
So if you think
about HSBC, again,

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.330
which is a very
traditional company,

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:08.390
they did everything right
for the past 20 years.

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:10.250
They started with
relational database.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:12.076
And then they
reached the limits.

00:15:12.076 --> 00:15:13.700
And they moved
progressively, actually,

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:17.090
to the traditional
data warehousing that

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.460
was variable 15, 20 years ago.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:21.200
And they found that
warehouses started

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:23.760
to be really expensive
and difficult to manage.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:24.800
So what have they done?

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:27.200
They moved to this
big data, actually,

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:29.230
technologies with Hadoop.

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:31.280
And they found it
extremely challenging,

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:33.320
because, suddenly,
they had, actually,

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:34.940
to learn about
these technologies.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:38.210
They had to learn how to feed,
actually, this technology.

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:40.600
They had to build
infrastructure.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:42.620
They had to maintain
infrastructure.

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:44.330
And it was extremely
challenging.

00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:45.830
So what they've
done, actually, they

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:48.542
started to figure out
that this is a data game.

00:15:48.542 --> 00:15:50.750
And they didn't want,
actually, to turn their company

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:54.410
into a competitor of Google
or any of our competitors,

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:54.910
actually.

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:57.210
They just wanted to
run their business.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.510
So they have decided
to select Google

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:02.360
to start this analytical
journey, this cloud journey.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:04.380
So they are moving
the bank to the cloud.

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:06.950
And they are moving the bank
to the cloud with Google.

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:09.660
So it's, again, a journey
we are just starting.

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:11.240
But it's really fascinating.

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:15.336
And we are seeing more and
more of these cases right now.

00:16:15.336 --> 00:16:16.710
So I'd like to
end on two things.

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.100
Number one, I really want to
thank all the partners that

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:20.120
are in the room.

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:21.890
You have an amazing exhibition.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:24.440
It's extremely important for us.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:26.870
If you want to be an
enterprise-ready organization,

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:28.640
which we are, you
need, actually,

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:30.170
to create this
partner ecosystem.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:34.610
So it's very close to our heart.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:37.610
I mean it's important to onboard
some technological partners,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:40.730
but also some resellers,
then also some global system

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:43.640
integrators that are
going to help you, again,

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:46.490
migrating your workloads
and also building what's

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:47.990
next in your organization.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.990
So spend some time
with these guys.

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:52.580
They have invested
in this event.

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:55.130
And they are able,
actually, to tell you

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:58.550
more about the value they can
bring to your organization.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.640
At the end of the day, again,
what I said, it's a journey.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:05.300
And very honestly and candidly,
I mean, it's really about you.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:09.050
And the kind of discussions we
are having with our customers,

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:10.970
I mean, are very interesting.

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:12.470
Because this is so new.

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:14.420
This is so new for everyone.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:16.670
How many times, actually,
we are sitting down

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.740
with our customers, and
they are just asking us,

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.180
how should I get started?

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:24.579
How do How do I do it?

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.349
Really, what do
I need, actually?

00:17:26.349 --> 00:17:27.970
Who should I recruit?

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.400
How can I find the talents?

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:33.580
What am I going to do with
my IT current organization?

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:35.740
How can I train these people?

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:38.150
How much is it going to cost me?

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:42.480
Am I going to be, again, locked
into a kind of partnership

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.190
or so-called partnership
with an IT company?

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:46.880
Do I have to rewrite my code?

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:49.840
I mean all of these questions
are really the questions

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:54.220
that we are trying
to help you answer.

00:17:54.220 --> 00:18:00.430
And this is something that Diane
Greene decided, very quickly,

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:02.480
when she came on
board two years ago.

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:03.750
She's our CEO.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.770
She was the founder of VMware.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:08.567
I mean, Diane said, we have
probably the best technology

00:18:08.567 --> 00:18:10.150
we can find, right
now, in the market.

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:11.980
But stop chest-beating here.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:13.370
It's not only about technology.

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:17.350
It's about the ability to build
an enterprise organization

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:20.890
and connect our technical people
to making sure that we can help

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:22.470
you building these projects.

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:24.610
So you'll see a lot
of Google Earth.

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:25.780
We call that Google Earth.

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:26.654
We have Google Earth.

00:18:26.654 --> 00:18:29.740
And in the room, you might
see some, what we call,

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:32.500
customer success
engineer, that will

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:36.665
help you piloting and
prototyping your application.

00:18:36.665 --> 00:18:38.290
You're going to see
a lot of engineers,

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:40.960
also, as I said, earlier
on, that will really

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:43.810
help you to drive the right
technical conversations.

00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:46.630
You will see some
dedicated architects

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:48.610
that are organized by industry.

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:50.950
We call them the
Office of the CTU.

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:53.990
It's like we have tens of
CTU in the organization.

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:56.110
And they are here, again,
to sit down with you.

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:57.790
And I'm so proud
and happy, actually,

00:18:57.790 --> 00:19:00.230
to have a lot of them
here, today, in the room.

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:03.550
So connect with these CTU.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:07.030
And also, professional services
organization, again, that

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:09.580
will help you and
help our partners

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:13.010
to implement and to
migrate your application.

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:14.570
So this is what it is about.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:17.440
We are super excited to
spend the day together.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:20.080
We have a lot to share with
you, a lot of product models

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:21.730
and customer stories.

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:24.910
And without waiting, I'm
going to pass it over

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:28.160
to Julien Blanchez, who is
going to tell us about security,

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:29.170
privacy, and compliance.

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:30.400
Thank you.

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:30.900
Welcome.

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:31.400
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:38.487
[DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYING]

00:19:38.487 --> 00:19:40.070
JULIEN BLANCHEZ:
Thank you, Sebastien.

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:42.110
I hope the audience will
cope with another 15

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:45.650
minutes of French accent.

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:47.540
Let's see if you can
spot, through the day,

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:49.760
how many accents we have
and where we come from,

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:50.801
each and every one of us.

00:19:50.801 --> 00:19:52.010
My name is Julien Blanchez.

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:54.590
I've been working nine years
with Google, the last four

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:58.100
years engaged with this
engineering, security,

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:00.860
and compliance
teams in California.

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:03.020
I recently relocated
back to Brussels.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:05.630
And my role, now, is to
engage with regulators

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:08.960
and large prospects,
large customers to help

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:12.650
answer the detailed
questions and facilitate

00:20:12.650 --> 00:20:14.780
the communication between
the European market

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:15.980
and these engineering teams.

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.340
So today, millions
of companies are

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:26.820
considering to move all or some
of their workload to the cloud.

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:29.660
And they have several
reasons to do that.

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:31.850
One of the reasons
that you might not

00:20:31.850 --> 00:20:37.130
have expected to be so
prominent and to actually

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:40.160
be the first one a cloud
provider talks about

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:41.870
is security.

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:45.890
And yet, it is a widely
accepted fact, today,

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:50.720
that large cloud platforms
offer a significant capability

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:53.600
to improve your security stance.

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:55.100
And there are a
number of reasons

00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:58.490
for that that I want to go
over in the next few minutes.

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:05.780
When you want to assess the
capability of a security

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:08.060
system, there is a
well-known framework

00:21:08.060 --> 00:21:11.690
that recommends to look at
the capability to anticipate,

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:15.830
the capability to defend,
and the capability to react.

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:20.570
When it comes to anticipation,
large cloud platforms,

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:26.030
like Google, have a
significant advantage.

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:30.680
As Sebastien was saying, Google
is operating eight products

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:32.630
with more than a billion users.

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:35.660
Actually Google Photo just
passed the billion user

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:37.890
mark a couple of weeks ago.

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:41.210
So this would add
one to this list.

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:43.440
So you can imagine the
amount of information,

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:45.080
the amount of
exposure, the amount

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:47.840
of testing we get
every single day

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:49.850
to know what is
happening on the cloud.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:54.530
And I just want to highlight
one of these platforms.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:57.380
Google Android, today,
is two billion devices.

00:21:57.380 --> 00:21:59.900
And Google Android does a
service called SafetyNet,

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:01.670
that you can Google if you want.

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:06.110
And SafetyNet polls, every
single day, 400 million

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:09.740
Android devices to gather
the security information,

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:12.020
from the devices,
and allows our teams

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:16.340
to build a close to realtime
cartography of the threat

00:22:16.340 --> 00:22:17.790
landscape.

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:20.750
So this gives you an idea
of the amount of information

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:23.090
and knowledge you
can benefit from when

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:25.970
you operate on these platforms.

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:29.810
Going on to defense,
our infrastructure teams

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:32.840
had the great
luxury, 18 years ago,

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:35.390
when they started to
build these platforms.

00:22:35.390 --> 00:22:39.080
They could start
from a blank slate.

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.510
They did not have to
cope with generations

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:45.290
of legacy technology that
is creating the complexity

00:22:45.290 --> 00:22:48.470
that, in one word, really
summarizes the security

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:50.000
challenge today.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.730
It is not to say that Google
is not complex, right?

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:56.900
Google has 2 billion lines
of codes, 20,000 to 30,000

00:22:56.900 --> 00:22:59.540
engineers, interacting
daily, onto 2 billion lines

00:22:59.540 --> 00:23:04.710
of code, 40,000 to 50,000 change
requests per day on the code.

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:09.050
So this complexity is
mastered by automation,

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:11.270
intelligent
automation, not to use

00:23:11.270 --> 00:23:13.850
the buzzword of machine learning
and artificial intelligence

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:14.630
here.

00:23:14.630 --> 00:23:19.430
But these systems have allowed
to tackle, successfully,

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:23.330
great security challenges,
like spam, for example,

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:26.240
and will allow to tackle
some of the big problems,

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:27.610
like authentication.

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:29.930
And I will be elaborating on
this more, this afternoon,

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:32.150
in the security breakout.

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:35.540
So when regulators, today,
are talking about security

00:23:35.540 --> 00:23:36.290
by design--

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:40.430
this is becoming a very
important criteria--

00:23:40.430 --> 00:23:43.590
Google has embedded
security into the system,

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:46.910
but, also,
intelligent mechanisms

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:49.550
that learn to adapt
to the evolving nature

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:51.500
of these threats.

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:56.210
Next, I want to talk
about the level of control

00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:59.930
that Google has managed to
keep over the entire technology

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:00.650
stack.

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:04.430
Because of the size we
operate at, from the hardware,

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:09.080
to the operating system, all the
way up to the user interface,

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:13.340
Google has kept a very
high level of homogeneity,

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:16.610
has developed and deployed
its own technology,

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:18.440
and kept this level
of homogeneity

00:24:18.440 --> 00:24:20.450
across the technology stack.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:22.340
This level of
homogeneity is what

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:27.470
allows us to identify threats
or incidents relatively faster

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:30.560
and react relatively
faster, because we have

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:32.750
less different
solutions to bring

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:35.970
and are less dependent
on external suppliers.

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:40.250
Now, in this time,
where notification reach

00:24:40.250 --> 00:24:42.710
and the speed at which
you can react and notify

00:24:42.710 --> 00:24:47.450
your data privacy authorities,
these kind of capabilities

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:48.800
are going to be critical.

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:52.610
And this is the kind of
strength that you really

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:56.100
want to have on your side.

00:24:56.100 --> 00:25:00.900
Then we should all be
aware of the importance

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:04.810
of a global infrastructure
in this security game.

00:25:04.810 --> 00:25:08.010
Today, Google is 15
data centers located

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:12.570
across the world, 4 of them
in Europe, Finland, Ireland,

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:15.300
Belgium, the Netherlands.

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:20.700
Two more coming before the
end of the year or very soon.

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:23.280
But most importantly,
these data centers

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:27.480
are connected by our
own proprietary fiber.

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:32.340
And this network
infrastructure allows us also

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:36.060
to deliver you very low latency
but, most importantly, when

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:38.700
it comes to security,
be much more in control

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:41.880
of our own destiny when it
comes to transport data from one

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:44.040
continent to the other.

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:47.730
More importantly, this kind
of global infrastructure

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:52.080
allows us to aim to deliver
the same level of security

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:53.970
for everybody.

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:56.730
You are not going to receive
more security, if you're

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:59.700
in Germany, then if you're
in China or in Pakistan,

00:25:59.700 --> 00:26:01.680
for that matter, or in the US.

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:05.550
So this is the capability
of these large platforms.

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:08.100
And, really, the mission of
the Google Security team's

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:12.210
to deliver the same level
of security to everybody.

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:15.930
Now these security discussions
can go on for days,

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:18.540
with large prospects,
weeks, sometimes,

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:21.510
if we really dig into the detail
and answer all the questions.

00:26:21.510 --> 00:26:24.250
They all end up to
the same conclusions.

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:27.420
Large cloud platforms offer
a significant improvement

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:30.270
over what most companies
can deliver today.

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:32.850
But they all leave our
prospect with the same feeling

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:36.690
that you probably have at
this moment when you realize

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:39.510
that, to benefit
from that security,

00:26:39.510 --> 00:26:44.490
you need to load your data onto
a third party infrastructure.

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:47.070
And this requires
a leap of faith.

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:50.070
This requires a level of
confidence that we cannot force

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:51.470
onto you.

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:55.100
All that we can do is
be more transparent,

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:58.580
share more information, tell
you where our data centers are

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:01.340
located, tell you
who are sub-processes

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:05.300
are, show you the dashboards,
public, live, of availability

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:08.810
of our systems, to allow you
to build that confidence,

00:27:08.810 --> 00:27:11.240
to come to sign the contract.

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:13.130
When you sign the
contract, this is

00:27:13.130 --> 00:27:15.980
when you really gain
legal control over

00:27:15.980 --> 00:27:18.560
how your data will be managed.

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:20.090
And you will be
looking for clauses,

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:25.100
like scope of processing, the
commitment that Google will not

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:27.440
use your data for
any other purpose

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:29.660
than deliver you the
service that you paid for,

00:27:29.660 --> 00:27:32.060
the commitment on
intellectual property,

00:27:32.060 --> 00:27:34.580
portability, the contractual
commitment that you

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:37.190
can leave the infrastructure
at any given moment.

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:39.990
How we will react to
a third party request

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:43.190
to access your data,
international jurisdiction

00:27:43.190 --> 00:27:45.550
will be also added
to these clauses.

00:27:45.550 --> 00:27:47.990
The commitment that we
will keep the security

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:50.630
certifications,
that we have now,

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:53.750
during the entire duration
of the agreement with you.

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:56.420
And finally, the
capability that you

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:59.240
have to verify
that we do what we

00:27:59.240 --> 00:28:02.300
say through audits
and the capability

00:28:02.300 --> 00:28:04.250
to audit our systems.

00:28:04.250 --> 00:28:07.700
Now, most of you and
most of your stakeholders

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:12.560
that you will have to go out
and convince, will be wondering,

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:16.700
how this can work within the
legal framework in Europe?

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:20.450
And, notably, with the
arrival of the GDPR,

00:28:20.450 --> 00:28:23.510
this new regulation that
is going to increase,

00:28:23.510 --> 00:28:25.940
dramatically, the power
of the data privacy

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:29.000
authorities to make
sure that we all

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:32.600
do what we should do when
it comes to handling data.

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:34.820
The deadline is coming close.

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:36.500
And that is why
we have announced,

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:39.380
several weeks ago, already,
our firm commitment

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:43.550
for Google Cloud, Google
Cloud platform and G Suite

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:46.940
will comply with the
expectations of the GDPR.

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:50.330
And you will be receiving, in
the coming weeks and month,

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:53.210
new legal terms
that really confirm

00:28:53.210 --> 00:28:55.480
that you have these
commitments that

00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:57.680
will allow you to
remain compliant

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:00.920
with your European regulation.

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:06.200
The GDPR is very specific
on one topic, which is

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:10.240
international data transfers.

00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:12.760
And Article 46 of
the GDPR is what

00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:15.176
you want to Google if you
want to have the legal text.

00:29:15.176 --> 00:29:16.300
I encourage you to do that.

00:29:16.300 --> 00:29:20.020
It is actually, for once,
a very easy, simple clause,

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:21.190
article to read.

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:24.430
Article 46 specifies,
very clearly,

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:27.280
that a data controller,
which is you,

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:30.460
or a data processor,
which is us,

00:29:30.460 --> 00:29:34.360
may only export European
data outside of Europe

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:37.450
if adequate safety
measures are in place.

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:40.010
And that is why,
back in January,

00:29:40.010 --> 00:29:43.060
we had our adequate
safety measures,

00:29:43.060 --> 00:29:47.110
as described in our standard
contractual clauses,

00:29:47.110 --> 00:29:48.400
added to our contract.

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:50.860
We had them verified
and approved

00:29:50.860 --> 00:29:52.780
by the European institutions.

00:29:52.780 --> 00:29:55.750
That clearly means
that, under the GDPR,

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:59.970
you will not be required
to keep data only in Europe

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:03.250
when you are using a
Google Cloud Platform.

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:04.330
You may do so.

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:05.560
You may not do so.

00:30:05.560 --> 00:30:08.060
You may use the
platform, as you wish,

00:30:08.060 --> 00:30:10.930
if you have these
contract clauses.

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:15.850
Now, very clearly, when
you engage into the cloud,

00:30:15.850 --> 00:30:18.130
you understand that there
is a significant portion

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:20.740
of the compliance
and security demands

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:24.160
of the regulators that are
going to be managed by Google.

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:27.430
And this portion will vary
depending if you implement

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:31.880
software-as-a-service,
platform-as-a-service,

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:34.360
infrastructure-as-a-service.

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:36.340
But even in the
green area, where

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:39.370
regulators and your
customers will be expecting

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:42.640
you to raise your
game and deliver it

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:46.030
to the new level of expectations
of the European regulator,

00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:50.980
Google technology can help
you achieve these goals.

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:53.890
And I just want to tease you
with a couple of the building

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:56.590
blocks that are available.

00:30:56.590 --> 00:30:59.840
And we can discuss,
further, during the day.

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:03.370
The strong
authentication ranks very

00:31:03.370 --> 00:31:07.630
high on the list of regulators,
today, and the list of hackers,

00:31:07.630 --> 00:31:10.090
today, when it comes to
assessing your capability

00:31:10.090 --> 00:31:11.530
to defend.

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:15.160
Rolling out these
security keys, that

00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:18.670
are a 2-step authentication
factor, hardware token,

00:31:18.670 --> 00:31:21.280
that we have rolled out at
Google, several years ago,

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:25.690
can significantly
improve your capability.

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:29.410
Enforcing their usage
is also a good idea,

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:31.360
just talking about
authentication.

00:31:31.360 --> 00:31:34.750
Another big topic
is anonymization,

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:39.100
the regulator requires you
now to anonymize and encrypt

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:40.270
the data.

00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:42.250
When using Google
Cloud technology,

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:44.800
your data is
encrypted by default.

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:46.990
This is a significant
differentiator

00:31:46.990 --> 00:31:48.580
versus other platforms.

00:31:48.580 --> 00:31:51.220
We will manage the
encryption for you.

00:31:51.220 --> 00:31:54.620
If you decide, you may
manage your own keys,

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:57.790
you may manage the
keys, yourself.

00:31:57.790 --> 00:32:00.310
Or you may bring your
own encryption keys

00:32:00.310 --> 00:32:03.620
to remain completely in
control of your data.

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:07.000
Finally, in the spirit of
[INAUDIBLE] Wood and Mark

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:11.650
[INAUDIBLE],, I want
to introduce Peter,

00:32:11.650 --> 00:32:14.800
who will demonstrate the
capability of the Data Loss

00:32:14.800 --> 00:32:20.680
Prevention API, which will
allow you to scan your own data,

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:24.790
to identify person
information or sensitive data,

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:28.630
sometimes even possibly in
realtime in your systems.

00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:33.550
And that can greatly help you in
your journey towards the GDPR.

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:34.540
Peter?

00:32:34.540 --> 00:32:36.250
PETER BAVINCK:
Thank you, Julien.

00:32:36.250 --> 00:32:39.760
I want to show you a short demo
of an enterprise application

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:43.610
that can be used in a
customer service organization.

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:46.130
So this is a chat application.

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:49.750
And in this scenario, we've
been chatting with Angela,

00:32:49.750 --> 00:32:51.550
solving her issue.

00:32:51.550 --> 00:32:54.580
Now, typically, in
such a chat, you

00:32:54.580 --> 00:32:56.440
would ask for some
personal information

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:59.230
to make sure you look
up her history, see

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:02.930
if she had other issues
before, and that sort of stuff.

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:05.590
So we asked for her name
and her contact number.

00:33:05.590 --> 00:33:07.340
And she did provide that.

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:09.460
But in the meantime, she
gave us so much more.

00:33:09.460 --> 00:33:11.950
She gave her insurance number.

00:33:11.950 --> 00:33:15.267
And she even uploaded a
picture of her passport.

00:33:15.267 --> 00:33:17.350
Now, that's probably not
the smartest thing to do,

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:19.300
but it gives us
a problem, right?

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:22.630
Because if we want to store
the content of this chat, which

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:25.510
is commonly done,
we have a problem,

00:33:25.510 --> 00:33:29.200
because it contains personal,
identifiable information.

00:33:29.200 --> 00:33:30.820
So we can't use it for auditing.

00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:32.290
We can't use it for analysis.

00:33:32.290 --> 00:33:35.330
We can't use it for training
purposes and so forth.

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:39.520
So what the DLP API provides,
it provides a mechanism

00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:44.290
to take the content of this
chat, run it through the API,

00:33:44.290 --> 00:33:47.500
and provide a redacted
version of this content, where

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:50.330
all that personal
information has been removed.

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:59.510
So let me respond to Angela,
thanking her and end the chat.

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:03.680
So now, all the content is
running through the API.

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:06.530
And what you will notice here
is that all the information that

00:34:06.530 --> 00:34:08.360
is personal has been redacted.

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:10.699
We see that her
name was taken out.

00:34:10.699 --> 00:34:14.900
We see that her phone number has
been removed, insurance number.

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:18.770
We even went into the
picture to take out her face

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:23.420
and remove any sensitive content
from the picture, as well.

00:34:23.420 --> 00:34:25.610
And to show you
that this is live,

00:34:25.610 --> 00:34:29.550
I have a small document
scanner set up here.

00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:32.300
I have a sample of
a Dutch passport.

00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:35.900
Its fake, so you don't
need to take pictures.

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:41.360
I can put it under the scanner,
click on the Capture Image,

00:34:41.360 --> 00:34:45.860
and it will run through the API,
and it will correctly identify

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:47.330
the [NON-ENGLISH] number.

00:34:47.330 --> 00:34:49.639
And it will take out the face.

00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:52.460
So that's an example of how
you can apply the Data Loss

00:34:52.460 --> 00:34:54.710
Prevention API in
your organization,

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:56.210
in an enterprise application.

00:34:56.210 --> 00:34:57.044
Back to you, Julien.

00:34:57.044 --> 00:34:59.168
JULIEN BLANCHEZ: Thank you,
Peter, very impressive.

00:34:59.168 --> 00:34:59.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:00.931
