00:43:22.160 --> 00:43:26.390
Now, we started with
Philips Hue back in 2012.

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:28.640
And from the very
beginning, we knew

00:43:28.640 --> 00:43:31.310
we needed to have
a cloud backend.

00:43:31.310 --> 00:43:34.840
We needed to be able
to keep our products

00:43:34.840 --> 00:43:37.050
up-to-date in the field.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:41.359
We needed to be able to
understand and learn,

00:43:41.359 --> 00:43:42.900
from how people are
using the system,

00:43:42.900 --> 00:43:45.940
to make it better over time.

00:43:45.940 --> 00:43:48.060
We needed to give the
ability for consumers

00:43:48.060 --> 00:43:51.725
to control their lights when
they weren't at home, so

00:43:51.725 --> 00:43:53.600
that they could have
the lights automatically

00:43:53.600 --> 00:43:55.350
be on when they
arrived home and be

00:43:55.350 --> 00:43:57.770
able to check that
they hadn't left things

00:43:57.770 --> 00:43:59.690
on when they were away.

00:43:59.690 --> 00:44:02.990
And we also knew we wanted
to connect our lighting

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:05.390
to react to other
cloud services,

00:44:05.390 --> 00:44:08.570
like if this, then that, so that
all of your lights in your home

00:44:08.570 --> 00:44:11.550
can flash red when your
favorite sports team scores.

00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:17.200
All of these told us we
needed a cloud platform.

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:21.690
But we also had some
unusual requirements.

00:44:21.690 --> 00:44:24.810
The way we actually
serve this cloud

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:26.490
service towards
our customers is we

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:29.280
bundle it for free in
the initial purchase

00:44:29.280 --> 00:44:30.912
price of the product.

00:44:30.912 --> 00:44:33.040
And what that means
is we actually

00:44:33.040 --> 00:44:38.234
have to bundle the lifetime
cost of serving that customer

00:44:38.234 --> 00:44:40.900
into that initial purchase price
as part of the bill of material

00:44:40.900 --> 00:44:42.280
of our product.

00:44:42.280 --> 00:44:44.440
So it's absolutely
crucial for us,

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:47.640
in order to keep the product
affordable for consumers,

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:51.850
that the incremental cost
of serving a new home

00:44:51.850 --> 00:44:54.490
is kept as low as possible.

00:44:54.490 --> 00:44:55.990
And that way, we're
able to actually

00:44:55.990 --> 00:45:00.160
make the purchase price
of the product cheaper.

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:03.010
We also needed to
make sure that we

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:06.980
could scale this product in
a very cost-effective way.

00:45:06.980 --> 00:45:09.490
We didn't know, when we
started, if this product would

00:45:09.490 --> 00:45:13.440
be hugely successful or
would take slower to ramp up.

00:45:13.440 --> 00:45:16.720
And because we started
as an internal start-up,

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:18.670
we didn't want to
be lumbered with

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:22.330
a huge operational overhead
keeping this thing running.

00:45:22.330 --> 00:45:24.910
But equally, if it did take
up very fast, which luckily it

00:45:24.910 --> 00:45:27.440
did, we need to be able
to keep up with scaling.

00:45:27.440 --> 00:45:29.800
Google Cloud solved this for us.

00:45:29.800 --> 00:45:32.290
And we're constantly,
pleasantly surprised,

00:45:32.290 --> 00:45:34.860
as the market keeps
growing faster and faster,

00:45:34.860 --> 00:45:39.129
that we're able to keep up with
scaling in a very smooth way.

00:45:39.129 --> 00:45:41.420
Another thing, which has
become increasingly important,

00:45:41.420 --> 00:45:45.000
is we care about latency.

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:46.919
We've been trained,
the last 100 years

00:45:46.919 --> 00:45:49.460
of having lighting in our homes,
that when you press a button

00:45:49.460 --> 00:45:51.850
the light turns on immediately.

00:45:51.850 --> 00:45:53.600
Why should it be any
different if you have

00:45:53.600 --> 00:45:55.320
internet connected lighting?

00:45:55.320 --> 00:45:59.720
So we put a lot of energy into
optimizing the total latency,

00:45:59.720 --> 00:46:02.390
from when you do an action,
which goes via the cloud,

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:05.006
to the lights re-acting,
becoming even more important

00:46:05.006 --> 00:46:06.380
with voice platforms
in our home.

00:46:09.950 --> 00:46:12.290
We have a very broad
partnership with Google.

00:46:12.290 --> 00:46:14.660
We use a wide variety of
different Google Cloud

00:46:14.660 --> 00:46:15.620
products.

00:46:15.620 --> 00:46:19.862
But we also work very closely
with the Android ecosystem

00:46:19.862 --> 00:46:22.070
and with the smart home
products in the Google thing,

00:46:22.070 --> 00:46:26.370
like Nest and Google Assistant.

00:46:26.370 --> 00:46:29.340
So let's have a quick look at
what it takes to actually turn

00:46:29.340 --> 00:46:34.890
on a light bulb in 2017.

00:46:34.890 --> 00:46:36.900
Let's start with a
command from something

00:46:36.900 --> 00:46:38.255
like Google Assistant.

00:46:38.255 --> 00:46:40.380
So someone in their home,
they get out their phone,

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:44.100
they say, OK, Google,
turn off my lights.

00:46:44.100 --> 00:46:45.090
What happens?

00:46:45.090 --> 00:46:47.390
That command goes up to
the Google infrastructure,

00:46:47.390 --> 00:46:49.950
does the voice recognition,
generates the command.

00:46:49.950 --> 00:46:54.990
And it comes with a client
request towards our API.

00:46:54.990 --> 00:46:58.870
The API is initially
managed by Apigee,

00:46:58.870 --> 00:47:01.000
where we're handling
that the command is

00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:02.920
properly authenticated
and within the quotas

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:04.720
of its application.

00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:08.470
That's then passed over
towards a service that

00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:13.570
runs in Google Container Engine,
called Client Eastwood, which

00:47:13.570 --> 00:47:17.110
handles that request, make
sure that it's connected,

00:47:17.110 --> 00:47:20.740
throws the right bridge, and
routes that message via a Redis

00:47:20.740 --> 00:47:23.030
instance running
on Compute Engine,

00:47:23.030 --> 00:47:27.700
towards another container,
which actually handles

00:47:27.700 --> 00:47:29.950
the connection towards bridges.

00:47:29.950 --> 00:47:31.510
The new way we're
doing this now is

00:47:31.510 --> 00:47:34.510
with a service we call Freddie
Mercury, which actually

00:47:34.510 --> 00:47:37.510
maintains a permanent,
open web socket

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:41.280
towards every single
bridge in the world.

00:47:41.280 --> 00:47:43.809
And we do that to get
latency as low as possible.

00:47:43.809 --> 00:47:46.100
There's always a connection
ready to take that command,

00:47:46.100 --> 00:47:49.340
so we can turn on the lights
immediately. it arrives there,

00:47:49.340 --> 00:47:51.230
and it's pushed
down this web socket

00:47:51.230 --> 00:47:53.570
towards the bridge
inside the home.

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:57.110
The bridge takes that and
sends out a Zigbee wireless

00:47:57.110 --> 00:48:00.590
message towards the lights in
the home, and they turn off.

00:48:00.590 --> 00:48:05.860
And all of this happens in
less than 300 milliseconds

00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:08.090
and happens millions
of times every day.

00:48:08.090 --> 00:48:10.460
And that's how we turn
on a light bulb in 2017,

00:48:10.460 --> 00:48:12.720
and how we're using Google
Cloud Platform to do it.

00:48:12.720 --> 00:48:13.496
Thank you.

00:48:13.496 --> 00:48:16.398
[APPLAUSE]

