00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:45.640
So as I mentioned
before, data is

00:48:45.640 --> 00:48:49.150
really key to success
in today's environment.

00:48:49.150 --> 00:48:51.510
And Google has really
been at the forefront

00:48:51.510 --> 00:48:54.270
of this kind of attitude.

00:48:54.270 --> 00:48:57.150
As you may have
heard, Google's motto

00:48:57.150 --> 00:48:59.640
is to organize the world's
data and essentially

00:48:59.640 --> 00:49:03.220
provide very, very high
level value to that data.

00:49:03.220 --> 00:49:04.860
We really mean that to heart.

00:49:04.860 --> 00:49:08.430
Google's culture is
fanatically data driven.

00:49:08.430 --> 00:49:11.040
All decisions are
made through data

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:12.900
driven argument and debate.

00:49:12.900 --> 00:49:16.770
It can be actually a little
bit maddening, sometimes.

00:49:16.770 --> 00:49:22.530
So this embrace of data
is really key, again.

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:26.800
And so in order to really
take the first steps--

00:49:26.800 --> 00:49:28.590
let's say I convinced you--

00:49:28.590 --> 00:49:30.690
you should probably
start by taking

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:34.740
care of your operational, your
transactional, your application

00:49:34.740 --> 00:49:36.884
database layer.

00:49:36.884 --> 00:49:39.300
And so I'm here to talk about
Cloud Spanner, which is just

00:49:39.300 --> 00:49:41.710
an incredible technology.

00:49:41.710 --> 00:49:43.660
It's amazing.

00:49:43.660 --> 00:49:46.800
So traditionally, when building
an operational application

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:49.720
database, you had
a binary choice.

00:49:49.720 --> 00:49:52.340
You wee probably
going to try to use

00:49:52.340 --> 00:49:54.560
a traditional relational
database, which

00:49:54.560 --> 00:49:59.610
has rich SQL semantics, strong
consistency transactions.

00:49:59.610 --> 00:50:03.420
But sadly, a relational
database doesn't really scale.

00:50:03.420 --> 00:50:06.360
You may hit some limits,
at which point, you perhaps

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:09.420
will choose to shard
your databases, which

00:50:09.420 --> 00:50:12.300
increases complexity
of operations,

00:50:12.300 --> 00:50:14.340
multiple times over.

00:50:14.340 --> 00:50:18.720
And so the NoSQL movement was
born, where it traded away

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:22.090
some of the really nice
benefits of relational databases

00:50:22.090 --> 00:50:24.450
in exchange for
horizontal scaling

00:50:24.450 --> 00:50:26.220
and in exchange
for availability.

00:50:26.220 --> 00:50:27.960
So you don't get
really nice SQL.

00:50:27.960 --> 00:50:32.580
You may not get
strong consistency.

00:50:32.580 --> 00:50:34.160
You might not get transactions.

00:50:34.160 --> 00:50:36.675
But you'll get scale, right?

00:50:36.675 --> 00:50:38.580
And Google
recognizes, of course,

00:50:38.580 --> 00:50:40.080
that this is kind
of inefficient,

00:50:40.080 --> 00:50:41.550
this binary choice.

00:50:41.550 --> 00:50:43.240
We can do better than this.

00:50:43.240 --> 00:50:45.610
So Cloud Spanner was born.

00:50:45.610 --> 00:50:47.400
And at Google, Cloud
Spanner has been

00:50:47.400 --> 00:50:50.480
in production, for the past
five or six years or so,

00:50:50.480 --> 00:50:52.720
running hundreds
of applications,

00:50:52.720 --> 00:50:56.010
including some of our
biggest ones like AdWords.

00:50:56.010 --> 00:50:58.010
So what is Cloud Spanner?

00:50:58.010 --> 00:51:01.530
It is the world's first
horizontally scalable

00:51:01.530 --> 00:51:04.370
relational database.

00:51:04.370 --> 00:51:08.690
Cloud Spanner provides strong
consistency of transactions,

00:51:08.690 --> 00:51:11.390
provides rich
standard SQL dialect,

00:51:11.390 --> 00:51:14.460
and has a very high
level of abstraction.

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:16.750
It's a fully managed
service, so that you're not

00:51:16.750 --> 00:51:19.220
inundated with tasks
like setting up

00:51:19.220 --> 00:51:22.210
infrastructure and
identifying how to shard keys

00:51:22.210 --> 00:51:23.340
and so on and so forth.

00:51:23.340 --> 00:51:25.010
So Spanner lets
you focus on what's

00:51:25.010 --> 00:51:27.110
important to your business.

00:51:27.110 --> 00:51:30.290
And again, of course, Spanner
is replicated regionally

00:51:30.290 --> 00:51:32.330
and, potentially, even
globally, which is

00:51:32.330 --> 00:51:33.785
a very new and novel concept.

00:51:36.510 --> 00:51:38.910
And of course, Spanner is
battle-tested at Google

00:51:38.910 --> 00:51:40.770
as I mentioned before.

00:51:40.770 --> 00:51:43.080
So how does Spanner
achieve this?

00:51:43.080 --> 00:51:45.299
It sounds too good
to be true, really.

00:51:45.299 --> 00:51:47.590
There are two components that
I should talk about here.

00:51:47.590 --> 00:51:49.290
The first one is TrueTime.

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:53.010
TrueTime is a software
and hardware server inside

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:56.850
of Google that allows you to
retrieve accurate timestamps

00:51:56.850 --> 00:51:59.550
within a non-zero error bound.

00:51:59.550 --> 00:52:03.330
TrueTime allows us to
serialize transactions

00:52:03.330 --> 00:52:05.047
with strong levels
of confidence.

00:52:05.047 --> 00:52:06.630
And that's really
the key to providing

00:52:06.630 --> 00:52:09.840
strong consistency
of transactions

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:12.150
that are spread geographically.

00:52:12.150 --> 00:52:14.790
The second one is a very
unique feature of Google,

00:52:14.790 --> 00:52:17.460
that some folks before
me may have mentioned,

00:52:17.460 --> 00:52:20.460
and that's Google's
private network.

00:52:20.460 --> 00:52:23.940
Google manages their packets
between data centers.

00:52:23.940 --> 00:52:27.150
It will take a packet from
data center A to data center B.

00:52:27.150 --> 00:52:29.520
And again, very high
levels of confidence

00:52:29.520 --> 00:52:32.610
that those packets
will make it there.

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:36.060
Those packets, your data
never leaves Google Network.

00:52:36.060 --> 00:52:37.890
And we're able to
really understand

00:52:37.890 --> 00:52:41.130
when any kind of
degradation happens there.

00:52:41.130 --> 00:52:42.810
And so the other way
to look at it is,

00:52:42.810 --> 00:52:46.720
you might have heard of the CAP
Theorem of distributed systems.

00:52:46.720 --> 00:52:50.620
The CAP Theorem basically says
that there are three aspects

00:52:50.620 --> 00:52:51.890
to distributed systems.

00:52:51.890 --> 00:52:54.840
There's the consistency,
availability, and partitions.

00:52:54.840 --> 00:52:57.570
And what they mean by partitions
is network partitions,

00:52:57.570 --> 00:53:01.380
which is just a nice way of
saying that bad things happen

00:53:01.380 --> 00:53:02.529
to networks.

00:53:02.529 --> 00:53:04.320
And that's really what
the CAP Theorem says

00:53:04.320 --> 00:53:06.150
is bad things
happen to networks,

00:53:06.150 --> 00:53:08.760
so you have to choose
between c and a,

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:12.940
between consistency
and availability.

00:53:12.940 --> 00:53:16.420
Google's private network
allows us to minimize the p

00:53:16.420 --> 00:53:19.350
down to seconds a year.

00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:21.730
In theory, we still have
the p to worry about.

00:53:21.730 --> 00:53:24.280
But in practice, the
p is minimized so much

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:25.720
that it's not a concern.

00:53:25.720 --> 00:53:29.680
So now you can have both
consistency and availability.

00:53:29.680 --> 00:53:32.230
And that's what
Spanner gives you.

00:53:32.230 --> 00:53:36.617
And now, I have Robert,
who has probably the best

00:53:36.617 --> 00:53:38.950
job at Google, because he
gets to play around with tools

00:53:38.950 --> 00:53:40.200
like Spanner every single day.

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:42.440
He's going to give us
a live demo of Spanner.

00:53:42.440 --> 00:53:43.900
ROBERT SAXBY: Hi, thanks, Tino.

00:53:43.900 --> 00:53:46.450
So all that said,
it sounds like it's

00:53:46.450 --> 00:53:49.630
going to be a lot of work to
get Spanner up and running, no?

00:53:49.630 --> 00:53:52.370
Well, we've tried to reduce that
to something very, very simple.

00:53:52.370 --> 00:53:55.570
So on this first screen-- and
I'll zoom in a little bit--

00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:58.660
we're actually going
to create an instance.

00:53:58.660 --> 00:54:03.722
So let's call it,
Next Amsterdam.

00:54:03.722 --> 00:54:07.360
And we're going to choose
here a number of nodes.

00:54:07.360 --> 00:54:09.270
So these are nodes of Spanner.

00:54:09.270 --> 00:54:11.540
And we're going to
go for 42, obviously.

00:54:14.860 --> 00:54:16.310
And that's it.

00:54:16.310 --> 00:54:17.450
I'm in the process now.

00:54:17.450 --> 00:54:19.670
And I've just
created and deployed

00:54:19.670 --> 00:54:23.319
my Spanner instance
with 42 nodes.

00:54:23.319 --> 00:54:24.110
That's really cool.

00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:27.830
But let's actually see a use
case and see what happens.

00:54:27.830 --> 00:54:29.930
So we're going to
switch to our next tab.

00:54:29.930 --> 00:54:31.160
So what's going on here?

00:54:31.160 --> 00:54:32.490
We have an application.

00:54:32.490 --> 00:54:34.340
It's been deployed
in three regions.

00:54:34.340 --> 00:54:37.100
So we have Europe,
Asia, and North America.

00:54:37.100 --> 00:54:40.550
And basically, we've got about
a billion tickets to sell.

00:54:40.550 --> 00:54:43.010
And we have 800,000 events.

00:54:43.010 --> 00:54:44.630
And what we're trying
to do, globally,

00:54:44.630 --> 00:54:47.444
is sell these tickets to the
customers who want to buy them.

00:54:47.444 --> 00:54:49.610
And obviously, we don't
want to sell the same ticket

00:54:49.610 --> 00:54:51.230
to more than one
person, so having

00:54:51.230 --> 00:54:55.737
a horizontal available database
is going to be really useful.

00:54:55.737 --> 00:54:58.070
So we've deployed Spanner in
all of those three regions,

00:54:58.070 --> 00:54:58.586
as well.

00:54:58.586 --> 00:54:59.960
And for this demo,
we've actually

00:54:59.960 --> 00:55:02.060
deployed Kubernetes
clusters there, as well,

00:55:02.060 --> 00:55:03.805
to actually simulate
this interaction.

00:55:03.805 --> 00:55:05.180
And as you can
see at the moment,

00:55:05.180 --> 00:55:07.490
we've sold about
10 million tickets.

00:55:07.490 --> 00:55:11.160
And we're about 600,000
tickets per minute.

00:55:11.160 --> 00:55:15.050
So let's dive down a bit.

00:55:15.050 --> 00:55:19.640
This is the schema that we're
using for our ticket service.

00:55:19.640 --> 00:55:21.860
And I think there are two
things to notice here.

00:55:21.860 --> 00:55:24.980
One, it looks like your
everyday, relational,

00:55:24.980 --> 00:55:27.860
normalized database
schema, which is great.

00:55:27.860 --> 00:55:29.000
Everyone can understand it.

00:55:29.000 --> 00:55:32.150
You can have a conversation
around it, with your analyst,

00:55:32.150 --> 00:55:34.340
with your developers.

00:55:34.340 --> 00:55:36.320
But let's look at one
of these tables here.

00:55:36.320 --> 00:55:40.940
So the ticket table is already
containing 1.8 billion rows.

00:55:40.940 --> 00:55:42.460
And that's in one table.

00:55:42.460 --> 00:55:44.039
That table isn't sharded.

00:55:44.039 --> 00:55:45.830
That's just one table
that you can look at.

00:55:45.830 --> 00:55:48.290
And it doesn't matter if you're
looking at it from America,

00:55:48.290 --> 00:55:50.030
Europe, or Asia.

00:55:50.030 --> 00:55:52.250
For all intents and
purposes, you're

00:55:52.250 --> 00:55:55.630
looking at a local table
containing one billion rows.

00:55:59.310 --> 00:56:02.400
So the next thing I'm going
to do is pull up a query

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:05.640
and actually run a
query against Spanner

00:56:05.640 --> 00:56:08.170
while we're doing
all of this work.

00:56:08.170 --> 00:56:12.370
So let me just go and grab
this query, which I prepared.

00:56:16.810 --> 00:56:19.136
So we should have a
query here for Amsterdam.

00:56:23.620 --> 00:56:24.410
Selecting that.

00:56:31.305 --> 00:56:33.430
So I'm querying the data
while all that other stuff

00:56:33.430 --> 00:56:34.140
is going on.

00:56:34.140 --> 00:56:36.640
And there's one thing I want
you to notice about this query.

00:56:36.640 --> 00:56:38.515
If I zoom in, that might
help a bit, as well.

00:56:41.930 --> 00:56:45.680
This is a pretty normal
looking SQL query.

00:56:45.680 --> 00:56:50.030
So something else that you get
with Spanner, normal, plain SQL

00:56:50.030 --> 00:56:51.412
interoperability.

00:56:55.270 --> 00:57:00.650
So the next thing
we're going to do

00:57:00.650 --> 00:57:03.122
is add some more nodes
to our Spanner instance.

00:57:03.122 --> 00:57:04.830
So you can imagine,
all this is going on.

00:57:04.830 --> 00:57:06.913
One of the things that
Spanner actually offers you

00:57:06.913 --> 00:57:11.540
is to make changes to
the schema, realtime,

00:57:11.540 --> 00:57:14.870
which would give you that
ability to actually go and add

00:57:14.870 --> 00:57:18.210
new features to your
system, your application.

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:20.270
But what about if your
application actually

00:57:20.270 --> 00:57:21.620
needs more power?

00:57:21.620 --> 00:57:25.410
Well, it's as simple as--

00:57:25.410 --> 00:57:30.390
zooming in again-- editing
the instance and saying,

00:57:30.390 --> 00:57:33.740
give me some more nodes.

00:57:33.740 --> 00:57:34.480
And there we go.

00:57:34.480 --> 00:57:35.771
We've just got some more nodes.

00:57:41.650 --> 00:57:43.940
Spanner, as with all of
our Google Cloud products,

00:57:43.940 --> 00:57:44.860
is fully integrated.

00:57:44.860 --> 00:57:46.189
It's a fully managed service.

00:57:46.189 --> 00:57:47.980
As you've just seen,
I'm not going in there

00:57:47.980 --> 00:57:50.730
and configuring things and
doing all kind DevOp stuff.

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:52.480
But what we've also
done is make sure it's

00:57:52.480 --> 00:57:55.421
connected with our monitoring
system, the Stackdriver.

00:57:55.421 --> 00:57:57.670
And I just want to pay
attention here to a few graphs.

00:57:57.670 --> 00:57:59.620
So one of the graphs,
this is the period

00:57:59.620 --> 00:58:01.930
when we started loading this
initial data into Spanner

00:58:01.930 --> 00:58:02.514
for this demo.

00:58:02.514 --> 00:58:04.721
And there are few graphs
that you can have a look at.

00:58:04.721 --> 00:58:06.610
So these are the nodes
going up and down.

00:58:06.610 --> 00:58:08.920
But this one here, when we
started to load our data,

00:58:08.920 --> 00:58:12.610
we were going about 200 to
300 megabytes per second.

00:58:12.610 --> 00:58:15.130
And we actually increased
that to get some more in,

00:58:15.130 --> 00:58:18.160
to like 600 megabytes per
second of loading data

00:58:18.160 --> 00:58:19.290
into our Spanner instance.

00:58:22.134 --> 00:58:23.912
And we're going to
come back to this

00:58:23.912 --> 00:58:25.120
and look at what's happening.

00:58:25.120 --> 00:58:27.326
We're going to zoom in
on America, a little bit,

00:58:27.326 --> 00:58:28.930
and look at the latency there.

00:58:28.930 --> 00:58:33.520
We're selling, there, about
200,000 tickets a minute.

00:58:33.520 --> 00:58:35.620
So with that, I'm going
to hand it back to Tino.

00:58:35.620 --> 00:58:36.712
That was Spanner.

00:58:36.712 --> 00:58:42.820
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:42.820 --> 00:58:43.570
TINO TERSHKO: Wow.

00:58:43.570 --> 00:58:45.710
What an amazing database.

00:58:45.710 --> 00:58:48.340
Just the fact alone, that
he was able to resize

00:58:48.340 --> 00:58:52.420
the size of a Spanner cluster
so easily, it's transformative.

00:58:52.420 --> 00:58:55.720
These types of things took
Google months and months

00:58:55.720 --> 00:58:57.720
to do before Spanner.

00:58:57.720 --> 00:58:59.740
And so you can take
advantage of this today.

00:58:59.740 --> 00:59:03.820
Spanner went GA roughly a
month ago, almost to the date.

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:07.840
And again, Spanner gives
you the best of both worlds.

00:59:07.840 --> 00:59:09.610
You get the nice SQL semantics.

00:59:09.610 --> 00:59:13.550
You get strong transactional
layer of relational databases.

00:59:13.550 --> 00:59:15.910
But you get the
scale characteristics

00:59:15.910 --> 00:59:18.671
and the availability
of NoSQL databases.

00:59:18.671 --> 00:59:20.920
So I, please, encourage you
to take a look at Spanner.

00:59:20.920 --> 00:59:23.590
It's really exciting.

00:59:23.590 --> 00:59:26.240
Now that we've taken care
of the application layer,

00:59:26.240 --> 00:59:28.720
now that we have our
operational, transactional

00:59:28.720 --> 00:59:32.710
database up and running, synced
across the entire world, let's

00:59:32.710 --> 00:59:36.310
talk about how to get some
more value out of this data.

00:59:36.310 --> 00:59:38.610
Because you really want to
understand your customer.

00:59:38.610 --> 00:59:39.790
You want to delight them.

00:59:39.790 --> 00:59:44.260
You want to have custom,
tailor-made experiences

00:59:44.260 --> 00:59:45.580
for each individual customer.

00:59:45.580 --> 00:59:48.190
And at the same time, you
want to be identifying

00:59:48.190 --> 00:59:51.100
opportunities, potentially,
for future exploration

00:59:51.100 --> 00:59:54.010
and future innovation
and future iteration.

00:59:54.010 --> 01:00:00.820
So really, the tool that
Google uses, internally,

01:00:00.820 --> 01:00:03.040
and Google customers
use, externally, as well,

01:00:03.040 --> 01:00:04.362
is BigQuery.

01:00:04.362 --> 01:00:06.820
BigQuery is a product that I
had the pleasure of working on

01:00:06.820 --> 01:00:09.040
before coming over
onto my new team.

01:00:09.040 --> 01:00:10.840
And BigQuery is,
essentially, a serverless,

01:00:10.840 --> 01:00:15.170
fully managed analytics
data warehouse.

01:00:15.170 --> 01:00:16.610
What do we mean by that?

01:00:16.610 --> 01:00:19.720
What we mean is that you never
have to touch hardware again.

01:00:19.720 --> 01:00:23.170
You never have to
resize your cluster.

01:00:23.170 --> 01:00:26.380
You never have to specify
any kind of configuration,

01:00:26.380 --> 01:00:28.570
like RAM, CPU, and so forth.

01:00:28.570 --> 01:00:30.910
BigQuery just works.

01:00:30.910 --> 01:00:32.410
BigQuery has two
major components

01:00:32.410 --> 01:00:33.451
that you can think about.

01:00:33.451 --> 01:00:36.910
The first one is a
sophisticated, exabyte-scale

01:00:36.910 --> 01:00:40.340
storage engine that allows you
to move data into BigQuery,

01:00:40.340 --> 01:00:44.170
in realtime, and share data
sets across your organization

01:00:44.170 --> 01:00:48.200
or with your third parties just
the same way Google Docs allows

01:00:48.200 --> 01:00:51.010
you to share information.

01:00:51.010 --> 01:00:53.760
Besides storage, we have
the execution engine,

01:00:53.760 --> 01:00:56.920
which allows you to
execute standard SQL

01:00:56.920 --> 01:00:59.350
at the speed of
thought, gives your data

01:00:59.350 --> 01:01:01.750
scientists, your data
engineers, your analysts

01:01:01.750 --> 01:01:04.020
the power to see
inside of that data,

01:01:04.020 --> 01:01:07.300
understand the
shape of the data.

01:01:07.300 --> 01:01:10.300
So customers, of
course, prefer BigQuery

01:01:10.300 --> 01:01:13.180
for its price and
performance characteristics.

01:01:13.180 --> 01:01:17.380
But they're most vocal about the
operational simplicity aspect

01:01:17.380 --> 01:01:18.190
BigQuery.

01:01:18.190 --> 01:01:21.650
It really is a set
and forget technology.

01:01:21.650 --> 01:01:22.500
It's really no ops.

01:01:22.500 --> 01:01:26.880
It requires no
workloads to manage.

01:01:26.880 --> 01:01:30.770
You just use BigQuery.

01:01:30.770 --> 01:01:33.685
But, of course, in order to
use BigQuery properly, just

01:01:33.685 --> 01:01:35.060
like any other
database, you need

01:01:35.060 --> 01:01:38.000
to move data into BigQuery,
which is potentially

01:01:38.000 --> 01:01:40.190
not the most glorious endeavor.

01:01:40.190 --> 01:01:41.840
ETL is hard.

01:01:41.840 --> 01:01:43.355
And it's kind of a requirement.

01:01:43.355 --> 01:01:45.440
It's a means to an end.

01:01:45.440 --> 01:01:48.980
So for that purpose, if you have
data sets that reside inside

01:01:48.980 --> 01:01:51.200
of Google, if you're
an AdWords user,

01:01:51.200 --> 01:01:53.660
if you're a DoubleClick user,
Google Analytics Premium

01:01:53.660 --> 01:01:57.290
and YouTube, we, on your behalf,
are able to move that data,

01:01:57.290 --> 01:01:59.850
automatically,
inside of BigQuery,

01:01:59.850 --> 01:02:03.860
so that your analysts can use
any number of holistic tools,

01:02:03.860 --> 01:02:06.890
like SQL or Python,
to analyze that data,

01:02:06.890 --> 01:02:09.660
together or with data
sets that you own,

01:02:09.660 --> 01:02:11.330
that you bring into
BigQuery, as well.

01:02:11.330 --> 01:02:14.002
And we'll be bringing in
other data sets into BigQuery,

01:02:14.002 --> 01:02:14.960
as well, in the future.

01:02:18.440 --> 01:02:22.210
So our clients tell us there
are really three major ways

01:02:22.210 --> 01:02:24.550
to use Google Cloud
Platform to derive value

01:02:24.550 --> 01:02:25.519
for your organization.

01:02:25.519 --> 01:02:27.310
The first one, that I
already talked about,

01:02:27.310 --> 01:02:30.410
is this cloud native
data warehouse concept

01:02:30.410 --> 01:02:31.920
that BigQuery holds.

01:02:31.920 --> 01:02:33.670
And it's really transformative.

01:02:33.670 --> 01:02:36.010
It's changing how
folks think about data

01:02:36.010 --> 01:02:39.660
and how folks think about
analyzing data, in realtime,

01:02:39.660 --> 01:02:41.260
in SQL.

01:02:41.260 --> 01:02:46.210
The second one is management
on Hadoop and Spark.

01:02:46.210 --> 01:02:50.110
It's 2017, it's been 11 years
since Hadoop first came around.

01:02:50.110 --> 01:02:54.130
And Hadoop is still very,
very hard to work with.

01:02:54.130 --> 01:02:55.960
It's a big headache.

01:02:55.960 --> 01:02:57.730
It's not unusual for
clients to tell me

01:02:57.730 --> 01:03:00.670
that it takes six to nine
months, on-premise, for them

01:03:00.670 --> 01:03:03.760
to get more infrastructure,
to get more storage

01:03:03.760 --> 01:03:06.010
or more compute
capacity, to increase

01:03:06.010 --> 01:03:09.350
the size of their Hadoop
clusters-- six to nine months.

01:03:09.350 --> 01:03:12.940
And at the same time, once you
do have capacity, sometimes,

01:03:12.940 --> 01:03:15.370
I'm being told that a
runaway query can take down

01:03:15.370 --> 01:03:17.200
the whole thing.

01:03:17.200 --> 01:03:20.410
And there are tools, like YARN
ResourceManager, that allow

01:03:20.410 --> 01:03:23.140
you to break up the resources.

01:03:23.140 --> 01:03:25.060
But it still doesn't go far.

01:03:25.060 --> 01:03:28.000
So Google's Hadoop and Spark
service, called Dataproc,

01:03:28.000 --> 01:03:31.150
allows you to spin up
clusters in seconds.

01:03:31.150 --> 01:03:34.240
To 98% of performance to
get a cluster up and running

01:03:34.240 --> 01:03:38.640
is about 60 seconds,
which is incredible.

01:03:38.640 --> 01:03:40.950
The third use case,
that I can talk about,

01:03:40.950 --> 01:03:43.684
is realtime analytics.

01:03:43.684 --> 01:03:46.320
Now, there is nothing
wrong with waiting

01:03:46.320 --> 01:03:52.146
a day or two until you get
analysis into your business.

01:03:52.146 --> 01:03:53.520
But there are use
cases where you

01:03:53.520 --> 01:03:55.530
want data analyzed in realtime.

01:03:55.530 --> 01:03:57.720
Philips Hue is a great
example of that, right?

01:03:57.720 --> 01:04:01.050
You want analytics
on your application

01:04:01.050 --> 01:04:02.200
as quickly as possible.

01:04:02.200 --> 01:04:04.710
So IoT, gaming, and
clickstream stream

01:04:04.710 --> 01:04:09.000
are three of the examples where
realtime analytics becomes

01:04:09.000 --> 01:04:11.080
really useful.

01:04:11.080 --> 01:04:15.622
Now, we've taken care of
the application layer.

01:04:15.622 --> 01:04:17.580
We know what the shape
of that data looks like.

01:04:17.580 --> 01:04:21.120
But to truly get the
most out of your data,

01:04:21.120 --> 01:04:24.410
you can start embracing
machine learning and AI.

01:04:24.410 --> 01:04:27.500
And this is a relatively
new concept, of course.

01:04:27.500 --> 01:04:29.180
Actually, it's a
relatively old concept.

01:04:29.180 --> 01:04:31.250
But because of the
advancements in computing,

01:04:31.250 --> 01:04:33.980
it's become a practical
concept, these days.

01:04:33.980 --> 01:04:36.230
And Google has really
attempted to democratize

01:04:36.230 --> 01:04:39.950
the concept of AI, through
our investments in TensorFlow,

01:04:39.950 --> 01:04:42.380
which is very popular,
open source project,

01:04:42.380 --> 01:04:44.150
through our
acquisition of Kaggle,

01:04:44.150 --> 01:04:48.230
which is a community of AI
and machine learning experts,

01:04:48.230 --> 01:04:51.260
and, of course, through our
fully managed machine learning

01:04:51.260 --> 01:04:53.810
service Cloud ML Engine.

01:04:53.810 --> 01:04:55.760
Cloud Machine
Learning Engine allows

01:04:55.760 --> 01:04:58.100
you to train your
custom TensorFlow

01:04:58.100 --> 01:05:02.180
models, on your data,
using our CPUs and GPUs.

01:05:02.180 --> 01:05:04.760
We abstract away the complexity
of running a distributed

01:05:04.760 --> 01:05:06.410
TensorFlow cluster,
so you can just

01:05:06.410 --> 01:05:08.780
focus on the really
difficult problem, which

01:05:08.780 --> 01:05:10.820
is machine learning.

01:05:10.820 --> 01:05:12.680
And once you have
the trained data set,

01:05:12.680 --> 01:05:14.780
or once you have
your model in place,

01:05:14.780 --> 01:05:16.490
you can take that
model, for inference,

01:05:16.490 --> 01:05:19.730
for future use on-premise,
onto a mobile device,

01:05:19.730 --> 01:05:20.640
or onto Google Cloud.

01:05:23.430 --> 01:05:25.610
Of course, we all
know that AI is hard.

01:05:25.610 --> 01:05:27.840
Machine learning
is very, very hard.

01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:29.006
Not everybody is ready.

01:05:29.006 --> 01:05:30.380
There's not a
whole lot of talent

01:05:30.380 --> 01:05:32.630
that really expertly
knows machine learning.

01:05:32.630 --> 01:05:35.270
To that extent, we
are externalizing,

01:05:35.270 --> 01:05:38.180
and we have externalized, a
number of pre-trained machine

01:05:38.180 --> 01:05:40.820
learning models that
power the same tools

01:05:40.820 --> 01:05:44.360
that Google uses, today, for
example Google Photos or Google

01:05:44.360 --> 01:05:47.060
Image Search or
Google SafeSearch,

01:05:47.060 --> 01:05:49.670
or, actually, Google Translate.

01:05:49.670 --> 01:05:52.370
So these same models are
being externalized for you

01:05:52.370 --> 01:05:54.770
to use, so that
any engineer that

01:05:54.770 --> 01:05:58.220
knows how to work with
a REST API or JavaScript

01:05:58.220 --> 01:06:00.650
can build an application
that can talk,

01:06:00.650 --> 01:06:02.840
that can be machine
learning-driven, AI-driven,

01:06:02.840 --> 01:06:04.200
and can talk to the real world.

01:06:07.060 --> 01:06:09.080
I'm going to talk about
a couple of these.

01:06:09.080 --> 01:06:11.500
The first one is Vision API.

01:06:11.500 --> 01:06:15.180
It's been available over
the past year or so.

01:06:15.180 --> 01:06:18.130
And we've actually annotated
over a billion and a half

01:06:18.130 --> 01:06:20.190
images by now.

01:06:20.190 --> 01:06:22.160
A couple of use cases--

01:06:22.160 --> 01:06:24.400
the first one is
real estate companies

01:06:24.400 --> 01:06:27.780
often take images of
real estate properties

01:06:27.780 --> 01:06:32.530
and extract metadata that
facilitates transactions.

01:06:32.530 --> 01:06:34.960
The second one is
social media companies

01:06:34.960 --> 01:06:38.890
are able to leverage the
Vision API to filter out

01:06:38.890 --> 01:06:41.760
inappropriate content.

01:06:41.760 --> 01:06:43.990
And the Vision API, just
like all of our services,

01:06:43.990 --> 01:06:46.090
tends to iterate and innovate.

01:06:46.090 --> 01:06:48.220
You kind of get better
and better features,

01:06:48.220 --> 01:06:50.380
more and more features
as time goes on.

01:06:50.380 --> 01:06:52.830
It's an intrinsic
quality of Google Cloud.

01:06:52.830 --> 01:06:56.860
Two of the most recent features,
that Vision API has introduced,

01:06:56.860 --> 01:07:00.190
are advanced OCR
and implementation

01:07:00.190 --> 01:07:02.620
of Knowledge Graph metadata.

01:07:02.620 --> 01:07:06.100
Advanced OCR, or Optical
Character Recognition,

01:07:06.100 --> 01:07:11.800
allows you to visualize or to
translate physical documents,

01:07:11.800 --> 01:07:13.930
like legal documents
or medical documents,

01:07:13.930 --> 01:07:16.570
translate them into text,
extract the text away

01:07:16.570 --> 01:07:18.160
from that.

01:07:18.160 --> 01:07:23.890
And Knowledge Graph metadata,
essentially, what Google did

01:07:23.890 --> 01:07:29.290
is we tied together the Vision
API with our Knowledge Graph

01:07:29.290 --> 01:07:30.670
metadata database.

01:07:30.670 --> 01:07:33.880
So that the same
metadata database

01:07:33.880 --> 01:07:35.760
that powers Google
Images, for example,

01:07:35.760 --> 01:07:37.430
you are able to use today.

01:07:37.430 --> 01:07:40.262
So it's just enrichment
of this Vision API

01:07:40.262 --> 01:07:41.470
with Google's internal tools.

01:07:44.350 --> 01:07:47.280
The next one is actually
just fascinating, as well,

01:07:47.280 --> 01:07:49.450
and you guys will
get to take a look

01:07:49.450 --> 01:07:53.170
at what this looks like live.

01:07:53.170 --> 01:07:56.230
The vast majority of data
that's being generated, today,

01:07:56.230 --> 01:07:58.630
in the world is really
videos, hundreds

01:07:58.630 --> 01:08:01.450
of terabytes every single hour.

01:08:01.450 --> 01:08:04.330
And it's very hard to
work with videos, right?

01:08:04.330 --> 01:08:05.620
You can't run SQL on it.

01:08:05.620 --> 01:08:08.150
You can't really store
it in a database.

01:08:08.150 --> 01:08:11.320
So what if you could
extract valuable information

01:08:11.320 --> 01:08:13.690
about your videos,
video content,

01:08:13.690 --> 01:08:14.860
in a very simple matter?

01:08:14.860 --> 01:08:16.760
And I have Lee to
demonstrate that for us.

01:08:16.760 --> 01:08:18.010
LEE BOONSTRA: Thank you, Tino.

01:08:18.010 --> 01:08:19.569
Actually, I am
very excited today

01:08:19.569 --> 01:08:22.270
to demonstrate the Video
Intelligence API, because it's

01:08:22.270 --> 01:08:26.569
really one of my favorite Google
Cloud products that's online.

01:08:26.569 --> 01:08:29.350
So I'll explain to
you first what it is.

01:08:29.350 --> 01:08:30.710
It is a machine learning.

01:08:30.710 --> 01:08:33.490
It's a pre-trained model
that we, as developers,

01:08:33.490 --> 01:08:35.830
can use through a
REST API, so you

01:08:35.830 --> 01:08:38.170
don't need to be
a data scientist

01:08:38.170 --> 01:08:39.399
or build the hard stuff.

01:08:39.399 --> 01:08:42.180
It's already there.

01:08:42.180 --> 01:08:45.100
And once you connect
to this REST API,

01:08:45.100 --> 01:08:48.640
machine learning can understand
the context of the video

01:08:48.640 --> 01:08:53.560
and also detect, per screen,
per scene, what's in the scene.

01:08:53.560 --> 01:08:55.590
And we'll label it.

01:08:55.590 --> 01:08:59.410
And also, we'll give you back on
which timestamp that happened.

01:08:59.410 --> 01:09:03.310
And I can show you
that in a real demo.

01:09:03.310 --> 01:09:10.359
I have, here, a video of
YouTube searches in 2016,

01:09:10.359 --> 01:09:13.960
where all things what
happened in the year 2016.

01:09:13.960 --> 01:09:15.279
So I'm scrolling through it.

01:09:15.279 --> 01:09:20.590
And you see like lots of people,
things of politics and sports.

01:09:20.590 --> 01:09:24.910
And if you scroll down, you
can see the Video Intelligence

01:09:24.910 --> 01:09:28.390
API detected all these
keywords, so everything

01:09:28.390 --> 01:09:30.439
that's happening in this video.

01:09:30.439 --> 01:09:31.990
Now imagine that
you have a company,

01:09:31.990 --> 01:09:33.970
you no longer need
to watch a full video

01:09:33.970 --> 01:09:36.040
to label or categorize a video.

01:09:36.040 --> 01:09:38.800
But you can let the
computer basically do that

01:09:38.800 --> 01:09:41.420
and also see what
time that happened.

01:09:41.420 --> 01:09:44.590
So if we can see here,
apparently, in this scene,

01:09:44.590 --> 01:09:46.100
there is a dog.

01:09:46.100 --> 01:09:49.240
And here is a
construction worker.

01:09:49.240 --> 01:09:51.939
I think that's amazing.

01:09:51.939 --> 01:09:54.850
Let's think about
another use case.

01:09:54.850 --> 01:09:57.280
And I think that's
probably real life.

01:09:57.280 --> 01:09:58.780
Let's say you have a company.

01:09:58.780 --> 01:10:04.060
And you have like petabytes of
video files on your website.

01:10:04.060 --> 01:10:10.300
And you need to find a certain
scene to highlight that.

01:10:10.300 --> 01:10:13.240
So let's try it that out.

01:10:13.240 --> 01:10:15.200
Since it's officially
summer today,

01:10:15.200 --> 01:10:19.640
I thought like maybe it's nice
to look for the word "beaches"

01:10:19.640 --> 01:10:23.910
and see if we have videos
that contain a beach.

01:10:23.910 --> 01:10:27.355
And now you see, these are
all videos with beaches.

01:10:33.030 --> 01:10:37.640
As you can see, you see,
also, on which time frame

01:10:37.640 --> 01:10:39.600
the beach area did happen.

01:10:39.600 --> 01:10:42.070
So if I could click here, then--

01:10:44.760 --> 01:10:47.700
let me refresh the page--

01:10:47.700 --> 01:10:49.350
then you should see
that, indeed, also,

01:10:49.350 --> 01:10:51.617
where the breach was
really happening.

01:10:55.820 --> 01:10:57.880
And that is actually
pretty amazing,

01:10:57.880 --> 01:11:00.570
because if I would
blink my eye, I probably

01:11:00.570 --> 01:11:01.500
would have missed it.

01:11:01.500 --> 01:11:04.080
So that is really great.

01:11:04.080 --> 01:11:06.374
Now, I had it over back to Tino.

01:11:06.374 --> 01:11:08.059
[APPLAUSE]

01:11:08.059 --> 01:11:08.809
TINO TERSHKO: Wow.

01:11:08.809 --> 01:11:12.230
