Drive Quick Access is
a mechanism by which we

01:18:36.700 --> 01:18:38.830
are looking at your schedule.

01:18:38.830 --> 01:18:41.470
We are looking at the
interactions you have in chat.

01:18:41.470 --> 01:18:43.090
We're examining the
things that you're

01:18:43.090 --> 01:18:45.400
doing, as you collaborate
with the people around you,

01:18:45.400 --> 01:18:48.340
and then we're surfacing
the content, that you need,

01:18:48.340 --> 01:18:49.930
at a fingertip,
when you need it.

01:18:49.930 --> 01:18:53.380
It turns out that, if
you measure it, over 40%

01:18:53.380 --> 01:18:57.070
of the documents that you need
are now being accessed directly

01:18:57.070 --> 01:19:00.760
from this new capability in
Drive called Quick Access.

01:19:00.760 --> 01:19:04.300
So when I open up Drive now,
and I look at the top bar,

01:19:04.300 --> 01:19:05.770
yes, there's a
Search bar up there,

01:19:05.770 --> 01:19:08.410
and, yes, it's very useful to be
able to do type-ahead searches

01:19:08.410 --> 01:19:09.700
to find information.

01:19:09.700 --> 01:19:11.830
But more importantly,
the documents

01:19:11.830 --> 01:19:14.890
that I'm most likely to
need are presented to me

01:19:14.890 --> 01:19:16.960
based on the context
and algorithms

01:19:16.960 --> 01:19:20.140
that we're building in and
using an artificial intelligence

01:19:20.140 --> 01:19:22.310
to surface this content.

01:19:22.310 --> 01:19:25.360
So that's an example of
giving you more time.

01:19:25.360 --> 01:19:28.030
It might be in
microseconds or seconds.

01:19:28.030 --> 01:19:29.740
But if you add that
up, what that's

01:19:29.740 --> 01:19:31.630
going to do across
the suite, is going

01:19:31.630 --> 01:19:34.570
to allow you to have more
productive thought-time, more

01:19:34.570 --> 01:19:38.600
productive opportunities
to engage with others.

01:19:38.600 --> 01:19:41.170
The second innovation,
that we brought to market,

01:19:41.170 --> 01:19:44.800
is the Explore
functionality within Sheets.

01:19:44.800 --> 01:19:48.520
I don't know about you, but I'm
pretty good with spreadsheets,

01:19:48.520 --> 01:19:51.250
but I'm not the guru that
can write a very, very

01:19:51.250 --> 01:19:55.510
complex statement that uses
all of the complexities

01:19:55.510 --> 01:19:58.840
of the spreadsheet language
to be able to search and find

01:19:58.840 --> 01:20:00.470
things in cells.

01:20:00.470 --> 01:20:03.190
Here's an example of a question.

01:20:03.190 --> 01:20:05.200
What is the most
frequent garment

01:20:05.200 --> 01:20:07.360
type, from the
point of sale data

01:20:07.360 --> 01:20:09.940
that I've been collecting,
over the last 10 years,

01:20:09.940 --> 01:20:14.170
analyzed by geography,
by location, by language,

01:20:14.170 --> 01:20:17.110
by segment of the market?

01:20:17.110 --> 01:20:19.840
When you ask that
question, how many of you

01:20:19.840 --> 01:20:21.820
can immediately write
the formula that's

01:20:21.820 --> 01:20:24.807
going to look at potentially
terabytes of information

01:20:24.807 --> 01:20:26.890
and be able to pull that
information up and put it

01:20:26.890 --> 01:20:29.260
in a graph in a
matter of seconds?

01:20:29.260 --> 01:20:31.240
That's not an easy thing to do.

01:20:31.240 --> 01:20:33.150
The best people that
know spreadsheets,

01:20:33.150 --> 01:20:34.790
that work with this
data, do it well,

01:20:34.790 --> 01:20:39.180
but the average person struggles
a bit with that formula.

01:20:39.180 --> 01:20:41.610
So you can go into
Explore, right now,

01:20:41.610 --> 01:20:44.660
and you can use the natural
language query processing,

01:20:44.660 --> 01:20:47.510
and you can simply
ask that question.

01:20:47.510 --> 01:20:50.120
What is the most
frequent garment type?

01:20:50.120 --> 01:20:53.120
And then you can add things to
it, by geography, et cetera,

01:20:53.120 --> 01:20:54.690
that I talked about earlier.

01:20:54.690 --> 01:20:56.300
And we will actually
not only show

01:20:56.300 --> 01:20:58.610
you the results of that
query, but we'll also

01:20:58.610 --> 01:21:00.390
write the formula for you.

01:21:00.390 --> 01:21:02.210
So if you want to
take that formula

01:21:02.210 --> 01:21:06.740
and drop it into the cells that
apply and do the summarization,

01:21:06.740 --> 01:21:08.180
you'll get that immediately.

01:21:08.180 --> 01:21:11.340
And now the next person that
wants to come into that Sheet

01:21:11.340 --> 01:21:13.910
and examine that
content, they do not

01:21:13.910 --> 01:21:15.559
have to be able to
write the formula.

01:21:15.559 --> 01:21:17.600
They don't even have to
know to ask the question.

01:21:17.600 --> 01:21:19.430
The information is
available to them,

01:21:19.430 --> 01:21:21.750
processed, and ready to go.

01:21:21.750 --> 01:21:23.780
So being able to
raise your game,

01:21:23.780 --> 01:21:26.260
raise your ability to
interact with the data,

01:21:26.260 --> 01:21:29.420
move to a natural language
processing capability

01:21:29.420 --> 01:21:33.320
is one of the outcomes that you
get from the application of AI

01:21:33.320 --> 01:21:37.870
and machine learning into,
in this case, Sheets.

01:21:37.870 --> 01:21:39.670
But how do we
enhance your ability

01:21:39.670 --> 01:21:41.134
to collaborate together?

01:21:41.134 --> 01:21:42.550
Well, there are a
number of things

01:21:42.550 --> 01:21:44.350
that we're releasing
in the product, now,

01:21:44.350 --> 01:21:46.780
and have recently
released to be able to get

01:21:46.780 --> 01:21:49.720
people to be able to
engage more proactively,

01:21:49.720 --> 01:21:51.860
engage in realtime.

01:21:51.860 --> 01:21:53.440
We've talked about Google Drive.

01:21:53.440 --> 01:21:55.390
And Google Drive is
going to be the next.

01:21:55.390 --> 01:21:57.640
We predict, by the
end of this year,

01:21:57.640 --> 01:22:01.930
that Google Drive will be
over the billion monthly

01:22:01.930 --> 01:22:04.060
active user point.

01:22:04.060 --> 01:22:05.560
And when we measure
an active user,

01:22:05.560 --> 01:22:07.120
that is not a registered user.

01:22:07.120 --> 01:22:09.250
That is someone who
is a seven-day active.

01:22:09.250 --> 01:22:11.560
Somebody who's used the
product in the context

01:22:11.560 --> 01:22:13.610
of the last seven days.

01:22:13.610 --> 01:22:15.910
So finding your information
and placing your documents

01:22:15.910 --> 01:22:17.360
in Drive, we think is good.

01:22:17.360 --> 01:22:19.430
But that, again, is not enough.

01:22:19.430 --> 01:22:22.690
So this year, we
announced Team Drive.

01:22:22.690 --> 01:22:26.740
Team Drive is that creative
space where I can declaratively

01:22:26.740 --> 01:22:29.890
create the space, I can add
the colleagues and people

01:22:29.890 --> 01:22:33.200
that are working with me
on a particular project.

01:22:33.200 --> 01:22:37.510
And then, now, what happens is
all the content that we create,

01:22:37.510 --> 01:22:39.730
all the content that
we interact with,

01:22:39.730 --> 01:22:42.220
and all the stuff
that we store belongs

01:22:42.220 --> 01:22:44.680
to the team not the individual.

01:22:44.680 --> 01:22:46.670
This solves a
number of problems.

01:22:46.670 --> 01:22:48.370
The first problem
it solves is what

01:22:48.370 --> 01:22:53.200
happens when a new team member
comes in to your existing file

01:22:53.200 --> 01:22:54.640
structure system?

01:22:54.640 --> 01:22:57.250
You have to go through and
do all the shares required

01:22:57.250 --> 01:22:59.650
to be able to bring that
person into the project.

01:22:59.650 --> 01:23:02.080
With Team Drive, we
eliminate that need.

01:23:02.080 --> 01:23:04.780
As soon as a new
person joins the team,

01:23:04.780 --> 01:23:07.210
they automatically have
access to the content

01:23:07.210 --> 01:23:09.610
that they need to be able
to collaborate with you

01:23:09.610 --> 01:23:11.372
and drive the project forward.

01:23:11.372 --> 01:23:13.330
The other problem we're
solving with Team Drive

01:23:13.330 --> 01:23:15.340
is what do you do
with the content

01:23:15.340 --> 01:23:17.840
when someone leaves
the organization?

01:23:17.840 --> 01:23:21.910
If you're using a traditional
file store, a NetApp filer,

01:23:21.910 --> 01:23:25.660
even a cloud storage
solution from another vendor,

01:23:25.660 --> 01:23:28.270
you have to go through
this process of trying

01:23:28.270 --> 01:23:29.920
to figure out, when
that person leaves,

01:23:29.920 --> 01:23:31.900
I'm going to eliminate
their ID, what

01:23:31.900 --> 01:23:33.910
is the disposition
of their content?

01:23:33.910 --> 01:23:34.770
Who do I give it to?

01:23:34.770 --> 01:23:36.130
Do I give it to their manager?

01:23:36.130 --> 01:23:38.110
Do I divide it up
among the team members?

01:23:38.110 --> 01:23:39.715
And how do I go
about and make sure

01:23:39.715 --> 01:23:43.270
that every single document
is appropriately disposed of

01:23:43.270 --> 01:23:45.340
and placed in its proper place?

01:23:45.340 --> 01:23:47.650
With Team Drive, you
don't have to worry about

01:23:47.650 --> 01:23:49.630
that when someone
leaves the team

01:23:49.630 --> 01:23:51.880
or leaves the organization,
the content doesn't

01:23:51.880 --> 01:23:53.560
belong to the individual.

01:23:53.560 --> 01:23:57.020
The content belongs to the team,
belongs to the organization.

01:23:57.020 --> 01:23:58.590
It's a better place
to collaborate.

01:23:58.590 --> 01:24:01.900
And it's more effective.

01:24:01.900 --> 01:24:04.980
We're also doing a lot in chat.

01:24:04.980 --> 01:24:09.270
Chat is a great tool that is
traditionally done one-to-one.

01:24:09.270 --> 01:24:11.220
Occasionally, you'll
get a new group chat,

01:24:11.220 --> 01:24:12.845
but you're not really
thinking about it

01:24:12.845 --> 01:24:14.700
in terms of a team
experience, where

01:24:14.700 --> 01:24:17.520
I have a repository
of interactions,

01:24:17.520 --> 01:24:19.950
that I'm having with the
team, and have continuity

01:24:19.950 --> 01:24:22.240
of the conversations
through time.

01:24:22.240 --> 01:24:25.140
So we are bringing to market
a new capability, which

01:24:25.140 --> 01:24:26.970
is Hangouts Chat.

01:24:26.970 --> 01:24:28.920
And Hangouts Chat
is designed to solve

01:24:28.920 --> 01:24:30.780
that problem of being
able to give you

01:24:30.780 --> 01:24:34.950
a team, an organizational
view of the conversations

01:24:34.950 --> 01:24:37.380
that you have, the informal
conversations you have to be

01:24:37.380 --> 01:24:40.150
able to move work forward.

01:24:40.150 --> 01:24:45.360
We also are greatly
enhancing our Hangout product

01:24:45.360 --> 01:24:47.140
and relabelling that
as Hangouts Meet.

01:24:49.902 --> 01:24:51.610
If you've used Hangouts,
you can see what

01:24:51.610 --> 01:24:53.443
a great tool it is to
be able to immediately

01:24:53.443 --> 01:24:55.090
connect to a number
of people, both in

01:24:55.090 --> 01:24:56.673
and outside of the
organization, to be

01:24:56.673 --> 01:25:00.370
able to have a video
collaboration experience.

01:25:00.370 --> 01:25:02.860
But what you need to make
it an enterprise capability

01:25:02.860 --> 01:25:05.950
is you need things like the
ability to record a meeting.

01:25:05.950 --> 01:25:08.230
How many times do you do
a team meeting or maybe

01:25:08.230 --> 01:25:11.950
an all-hands call, where you're
giving out important content,

01:25:11.950 --> 01:25:13.690
but there are people
that are on holiday,

01:25:13.690 --> 01:25:15.231
there are people
that are sick, there

01:25:15.231 --> 01:25:17.920
are people that have conflicting
meetings and can't make it?

01:25:17.920 --> 01:25:21.130
You need to be able to have
the ability to record content.

01:25:21.130 --> 01:25:23.140
You also need to make
it seamless for people

01:25:23.140 --> 01:25:24.880
to be able to get
into the meeting

01:25:24.880 --> 01:25:27.550
regardless of what device
or what location they are

01:25:27.550 --> 01:25:29.800
and what the power
of their network is.

01:25:29.800 --> 01:25:32.290
So why shouldn't
every single meeting

01:25:32.290 --> 01:25:35.440
have a bridge, a
phone bridge, where,

01:25:35.440 --> 01:25:38.810
if I'm in a low
bandwidth location,

01:25:38.810 --> 01:25:41.080
and it's not possible for
me to get the best out

01:25:41.080 --> 01:25:44.140
of my IP network, why shouldn't
I automatically offer you

01:25:44.140 --> 01:25:47.410
the ability to dial into the
bridge and just click a button

01:25:47.410 --> 01:25:49.210
and be able to be
connected into that?

01:25:49.210 --> 01:25:52.000
Why can I connect other
people without having

01:25:52.000 --> 01:25:56.710
to type in the conference bridge
number, type in the leader

01:25:56.710 --> 01:25:58.870
code, go through all
this sequence of numbers,

01:25:58.870 --> 01:26:02.620
realizing that you mistyped the
5th digit of a 15-digit number,

01:26:02.620 --> 01:26:04.690
and all the hassle
associated with joining

01:26:04.690 --> 01:26:05.830
a meeting that way?

01:26:05.830 --> 01:26:07.120
It should be one click.

01:26:07.120 --> 01:26:08.290
It should be automatic.

01:26:08.290 --> 01:26:11.410
It should be accessible
from mobile, from desktop,

01:26:11.410 --> 01:26:13.259
and from any location
that you're in.

01:26:13.259 --> 01:26:14.800
So we're doing a
lot in Hangouts Meet

01:26:14.800 --> 01:26:18.370
to be able to bring the power
of Hangouts to the enterprise.

01:26:18.370 --> 01:26:20.260
You also need to
increase the number

01:26:20.260 --> 01:26:21.800
of people that are interacting.

01:26:21.800 --> 01:26:25.381
So we're going and increasing
that number every single year

01:26:25.381 --> 01:26:27.880
to be able to make it so you
can have large meetings as well

01:26:27.880 --> 01:26:31.230
as intimate, small meetings.

01:26:31.230 --> 01:26:33.840
So that's we're doing, an
example of some of the things

01:26:33.840 --> 01:26:36.510
we're doing around
team collaboration

01:26:36.510 --> 01:26:37.680
and getting people together.

01:26:37.680 --> 01:26:39.270
What about that fourth place?

01:26:39.270 --> 01:26:43.050
What about the integration
of your workplace?

01:26:43.050 --> 01:26:45.120
When I'm in email,
and I need to be

01:26:45.120 --> 01:26:46.680
able to get to
another application,

01:26:46.680 --> 01:26:47.780
I can do it quickly.

01:26:47.780 --> 01:26:50.220
What about actually
building applications that

01:26:50.220 --> 01:26:53.430
use these capabilities
to be able to provide

01:26:53.430 --> 01:26:55.530
new services to
your organization

01:26:55.530 --> 01:26:57.840
and to your customers
and partners?

01:26:57.840 --> 01:27:01.260
Well, the traditional
development strategy

01:27:01.260 --> 01:27:04.740
is great if you're a computer
science software engineer.

01:27:04.740 --> 01:27:07.680
So if you're a Python
programmer, a Java programmer,

01:27:07.680 --> 01:27:09.900
and you want to go in and
write a complex system

01:27:09.900 --> 01:27:13.990
and use all the power that
the cloud has to offer,

01:27:13.990 --> 01:27:15.790
that's wonderful.

01:27:15.790 --> 01:27:18.690
But what if you're a
power business user?

01:27:18.690 --> 01:27:22.470
Where has the market left you
in terms of building a dynamic

01:27:22.470 --> 01:27:25.910
and active application built
on top of this infrastructure

01:27:25.910 --> 01:27:27.780
and built on
platform-as-a-service to where

01:27:27.780 --> 01:27:30.810
I can build a simple
application, as a power user,

01:27:30.810 --> 01:27:34.350
deploy that into Google Cloud,
and have it automatically scale

01:27:34.350 --> 01:27:38.260
to billions of users, should
I need that capability?

01:27:38.260 --> 01:27:42.180
Well, I'd like to
introduce you to a platform

01:27:42.180 --> 01:27:44.640
and a development
capability called App Maker.

01:27:44.640 --> 01:27:46.560
And I think the best
way to introduce you

01:27:46.560 --> 01:27:49.140
to that is to show
you a video of people

01:27:49.140 --> 01:27:51.955
that are using it today and the
experience that they've had.

01:27:51.955 --> 01:27:52.830
Let's roll the video.

01:27:52.830 --> 01:27:58.490
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:27:58.490 --> 01:28:01.290
- You're on Google, and you're
enjoying the collaboration

01:28:01.290 --> 01:28:03.630
products, Mail,
Calendar, Contacts.

01:28:03.630 --> 01:28:06.210
However, you also have this
need for a business process

01:28:06.210 --> 01:28:07.620
to be automated.

01:28:07.620 --> 01:28:09.720
- Our agency, they
don't have the money

01:28:09.720 --> 01:28:11.340
to do a full fledged app.

01:28:11.340 --> 01:28:14.520
They just need something simple
that makes their life easier.

01:28:14.520 --> 01:28:15.960
Right now, we have
the blue form,

01:28:15.960 --> 01:28:17.418
because it's a blue
sheet of paper.

01:28:17.418 --> 01:28:19.496
- The Wyoming Flexible
Benefits Enrollment Plan.

01:28:19.496 --> 01:28:21.120
- You're filling it
out and signing it,

01:28:21.120 --> 01:28:22.770
and then they have
to data entry all

01:28:22.770 --> 01:28:27.030
that back in, 10,000
people, a lot of blue trees.

01:28:27.030 --> 01:28:29.040
- We were able to make
this in App Maker.

01:28:29.040 --> 01:28:30.840
- We showed it to
our HR department.

01:28:30.840 --> 01:28:33.450
And they were so excited.

01:28:33.450 --> 01:28:35.370
- Honestly, at first,
I was very skeptical.

01:28:35.370 --> 01:28:36.720
It just seemed too easy.

01:28:36.720 --> 01:28:39.005
Like, oh, yeah, I drag and
drop a couple of things.

01:28:39.005 --> 01:28:39.630
What do I care?

01:28:39.630 --> 01:28:40.560
I'm a real developer.

01:28:40.560 --> 01:28:42.090
I don't use these
little gadgets.

01:28:42.090 --> 01:28:44.760
But as I got to use the
tool more, I was like, wow,

01:28:44.760 --> 01:28:48.190
you know this really can
do some pretty cool stuff.

01:28:48.190 --> 01:28:50.820
I was able to develop a
relatively sophisticated app

01:28:50.820 --> 01:28:52.690
for login monitoring.

01:28:52.690 --> 01:28:55.860
From ideation, all the way
through the final delivered

01:28:55.860 --> 01:28:59.311
app, to our administrators, for
them to use, was five weeks.

01:28:59.311 --> 01:29:01.810
And that's just unheard of in
any other kind of product life

01:29:01.810 --> 01:29:03.346
cycle.

01:29:03.346 --> 01:29:04.890
- Why App Maker?

01:29:04.890 --> 01:29:08.310
Because not everyone is me.

01:29:08.310 --> 01:29:12.300
I really like working deep down
in the code, working low level.

01:29:12.300 --> 01:29:14.310
The great thing
about App Maker is

01:29:14.310 --> 01:29:16.890
you don't need to have
that deep down knowledge.

01:29:16.890 --> 01:29:19.080
People who are
able to make these

01:29:19.080 --> 01:29:21.750
really snazzy looking
applications that can

01:29:21.750 --> 01:29:23.940
be used by an entire company.

01:29:23.940 --> 01:29:26.490
- I never pictured myself
developing anything.

01:29:26.490 --> 01:29:31.835
App Maker is an insanely awesome
way to solve a small problem.

01:29:31.835 --> 01:29:35.195
- App Maker makes my
job easier, because I

01:29:35.195 --> 01:29:37.140
don't need multiple tools.

01:29:37.140 --> 01:29:41.830
I can develop it,
directly, on App Maker.

01:29:41.830 --> 01:29:44.030
- One word to
describe App Maker?

01:29:44.030 --> 01:29:44.600
Simple.

01:29:44.600 --> 01:29:45.230
- Quick.

01:29:45.230 --> 01:29:45.830
- Flexible.

01:29:45.830 --> 01:29:46.520
- Versatile.

01:29:46.520 --> 01:29:47.300
- Empowerment.

01:29:47.300 --> 01:29:49.849
- One word to
describe App Maker?

01:29:49.849 --> 01:29:50.390
Game changer.

01:29:57.242 --> 01:29:58.340
[END PLAYBACK]

01:29:58.340 --> 01:30:00.890
BILL HIPPENMEYER: So App
Maker creates the development

01:30:00.890 --> 01:30:03.500
environment with which you
can build simple applications

01:30:03.500 --> 01:30:05.750
that scale to billions
of users on top

01:30:05.750 --> 01:30:07.250
of Google infrastructure.

01:30:07.250 --> 01:30:10.130
And it sits on top of
a rich set of APIs.

01:30:10.130 --> 01:30:12.540
So not only can you
build applications,

01:30:12.540 --> 01:30:14.840
but you can also build
applications that integrate

01:30:14.840 --> 01:30:16.490
your collaboration environment.

01:30:16.490 --> 01:30:20.030
So we've announced the
Spreadsheets API and the Slides

01:30:20.030 --> 01:30:23.570
API as examples
of APIs that allow

01:30:23.570 --> 01:30:25.580
you to start to
programmatically create

01:30:25.580 --> 01:30:28.614
content, programmatically
manipulate slide decks

01:30:28.614 --> 01:30:31.280
and do those types of things, so
that you can actually integrate

01:30:31.280 --> 01:30:32.780
these types of workflows.

01:30:32.780 --> 01:30:34.940
In addition to that,
we've also introduced

01:30:34.940 --> 01:30:37.910
the concept of Gmail AddOns.

01:30:37.910 --> 01:30:40.910
When you're working in the
context of your email inbox,

01:30:40.910 --> 01:30:43.010
that is where I get
a lot of my to-dos.

01:30:43.010 --> 01:30:45.930
That is where I'm actually
wanting to do most of my work.

01:30:45.930 --> 01:30:49.220
So in the morning, I might
get, for example, a customer

01:30:49.220 --> 01:30:51.650
complaint or an
issue or a concern

01:30:51.650 --> 01:30:53.300
or some sort of escalation.

01:30:53.300 --> 01:30:56.270
In that might be
a trouble ticket.

01:30:56.270 --> 01:30:59.330
I don't want to have to leave
the context of my email,

01:30:59.330 --> 01:31:01.940
go in and log into the
trouble ticketing system,

01:31:01.940 --> 01:31:03.500
look up the reference
to the trouble,

01:31:03.500 --> 01:31:05.930
find out what's been done on
that ticket in the last 24

01:31:05.930 --> 01:31:08.330
hours, contact the
engineer that's working

01:31:08.330 --> 01:31:09.830
on it to get information.

01:31:09.830 --> 01:31:12.860
Why can't I simply integrate
that experience, where

01:31:12.860 --> 01:31:17.000
I can click on the ticket number
and, through Gmail AddOns,

01:31:17.000 --> 01:31:19.010
surface the
application directly,

01:31:19.010 --> 01:31:21.080
in the context of
the work I'm doing,

01:31:21.080 --> 01:31:23.150
go and look the
information up I need,

01:31:23.150 --> 01:31:25.070
grab some of that
information, stick it back

01:31:25.070 --> 01:31:28.040
into the email reply,
and reduce the time

01:31:28.040 --> 01:31:32.480
it takes to effectively
resolve a customer concern?

01:31:32.480 --> 01:31:35.750
If you add those up, over
the course of your year,

01:31:35.750 --> 01:31:39.080
you begin to free up, you begin
to work more collaboratively.

01:31:39.080 --> 01:31:42.020
You're able to actually leverage
the power of a collaboration

01:31:42.020 --> 01:31:45.060
solution that was
built for teams.

01:31:45.060 --> 01:31:46.580
So we have a bunch
of technology,

01:31:46.580 --> 01:31:48.500
here, in the
collaboration suite.

01:31:48.500 --> 01:31:50.060
We think it's differentiated.

01:31:50.060 --> 01:31:52.130
And we think it will
help you do what?

01:31:52.130 --> 01:31:54.590
We think our collaboration
suite uniquely

01:31:54.590 --> 01:31:58.010
helps you achieve the
transformative outcomes you're

01:31:58.010 --> 01:32:02.360
doing, based on coming up with
big ideas, which is fostered

01:32:02.360 --> 01:32:06.560
by a creative environment, which
is catalyzed by your ability

01:32:06.560 --> 01:32:08.486
to engage with others.

01:32:08.486 --> 01:32:09.860
So with that, what
I'd like to do

01:32:09.860 --> 01:32:11.990
is take you to the final round.

01:32:11.990 --> 01:32:14.000
In the US, we would
say, in baseball, we're

01:32:14.000 --> 01:32:16.650
heading around third,
and we're going home now.

01:32:16.650 --> 01:32:18.740
And to do that, I
would like to bring up

01:32:18.740 --> 01:32:21.800
Andre, who is going to talk
a little bit about what we're

01:32:21.800 --> 01:32:24.530
doing to maintain
an open environment,

01:32:24.530 --> 01:32:27.140
so that you can make sure
that, when you build things

01:32:27.140 --> 01:32:29.750
on our platform, they're
going to run in other places,

01:32:29.750 --> 01:32:32.550
and that you're going to
work in an open platform.

01:32:32.550 --> 01:32:33.470
So Andre?

