01:33:29.250 --> 01:33:32.010
I'm really privileged
to have seen that.

01:33:32.010 --> 01:33:35.340
One of the reasons
that I joined Google

01:33:35.340 --> 01:33:38.190
is Google's vision on openness.

01:33:38.190 --> 01:33:42.499
And today, I would like to speak
with you especially about that.

01:33:42.499 --> 01:33:44.040
And there's three
pieces I would like

01:33:44.040 --> 01:33:48.660
to touch here, one, Google's
support for open source

01:33:48.660 --> 01:33:52.290
and how Google believes that
it will speed up innovation.

01:33:52.290 --> 01:33:56.670
The second is how we are
open to other technologies,

01:33:56.670 --> 01:33:59.490
and third,
flexibility in pricing

01:33:59.490 --> 01:34:04.350
and how Google believes that
pricing should be flexible

01:34:04.350 --> 01:34:08.230
and drive waste down to zero.

01:34:08.230 --> 01:34:12.860
Imagine that you
are in a new market.

01:34:12.860 --> 01:34:16.580
And you are in a new market,
one way or the other, everybody,

01:34:16.580 --> 01:34:17.580
here, right?

01:34:17.580 --> 01:34:19.250
The next big thing--

01:34:19.250 --> 01:34:22.130
And your competition
has this three year

01:34:22.130 --> 01:34:24.830
or multi-year head-start
in front of you.

01:34:24.830 --> 01:34:26.880
What are you going to do?

01:34:26.880 --> 01:34:28.520
How are you going to compete?

01:34:28.520 --> 01:34:31.760
Well, you might think
of Google Cloud, maybe.

01:34:31.760 --> 01:34:33.410
Now, this happened to Linux.

01:34:33.410 --> 01:34:38.420
And 20 years ago, the internet,
that was the next big thing.

01:34:38.420 --> 01:34:43.970
And honest, Linux was not such
a great platform in those days.

01:34:43.970 --> 01:34:45.090
Yahoo was really big.

01:34:45.090 --> 01:34:47.510
They had their own
operating system.

01:34:47.510 --> 01:34:50.060
Solaris was super,
super popular.

01:34:50.060 --> 01:34:52.460
And we all know what happened.

01:34:52.460 --> 01:34:58.610
Unix became the platform of the
internet, because it's open.

01:34:58.610 --> 01:35:02.060
Now, really, because
it's open, because it had

01:35:02.060 --> 01:35:05.960
a higher rate of innovation.

01:35:05.960 --> 01:35:09.650
Google has a longstanding
history of supporting open

01:35:09.650 --> 01:35:16.000
source systems, Linux,
Git, C++, many of those.

01:35:16.000 --> 01:35:17.370
That is all anecdotal, right?

01:35:17.370 --> 01:35:18.980
So let's look at some numbers.

01:35:18.980 --> 01:35:27.630
Over 280,000 commits, from
Google.com accounts, in 1 year,

01:35:27.630 --> 01:35:28.580
2016.

01:35:28.580 --> 01:35:30.430
So if you do the math,
it's 700 per day.

01:35:30.430 --> 01:35:32.320
That's super amazing, right?

01:35:32.320 --> 01:35:38.770
15,000 projects that Google
is supporting, over 2,500 long

01:35:38.770 --> 01:35:41.440
during projects going on.

01:35:41.440 --> 01:35:44.820
And there's another piece,
here, open source is steering

01:35:44.820 --> 01:35:47.550
a community of high
collaboration and

01:35:47.550 --> 01:35:50.970
of high innovation and
high speeds of innovation.

01:35:50.970 --> 01:35:52.780
Let's look at an example.

01:35:52.780 --> 01:35:56.550
What started out as Googlers
adding functionality

01:35:56.550 --> 01:36:01.230
to the C kernel of Linux,
to just manage resources,

01:36:01.230 --> 01:36:03.960
like CPU sets and
other resources,

01:36:03.960 --> 01:36:09.540
grew into what we know now
as containers, microservices.

01:36:09.540 --> 01:36:13.050
And 12 years later,
now, companies

01:36:13.050 --> 01:36:14.760
around to world,
people around the world

01:36:14.760 --> 01:36:17.970
are speeding up application
development and DevOps

01:36:17.970 --> 01:36:20.610
through containers.

01:36:20.610 --> 01:36:24.330
And at Google, we use
that, of course, also.

01:36:24.330 --> 01:36:26.289
And containers, in
itself, are great.

01:36:26.289 --> 01:36:28.080
Containers also need
to be managed and need

01:36:28.080 --> 01:36:29.470
to be orchestrated, right?

01:36:29.470 --> 01:36:34.440
So in Google, we have
this humongous robot

01:36:34.440 --> 01:36:36.000
that we lovingly call Borg.

01:36:36.000 --> 01:36:39.060
And I know, we make the jokes.

01:36:39.060 --> 01:36:41.220
Resistance is futile, right?

01:36:41.220 --> 01:36:45.550
And Borg, we made a
super bold decision.

01:36:45.550 --> 01:36:48.780
Borg, by the way, in
Google is orchestrating

01:36:48.780 --> 01:36:51.120
over 2 billion
microservices a day.

01:36:51.120 --> 01:36:54.000
So it is quite a
humongous robot.

01:36:54.000 --> 01:36:55.530
And we made a bold decision.

01:36:55.530 --> 01:36:59.130
What we did is we
open sourced Borg.

01:36:59.130 --> 01:37:02.190
And we gave the code
and the management of IP

01:37:02.190 --> 01:37:04.620
to the Linux Foundation.

01:37:04.620 --> 01:37:08.580
And there Kubernetes was born.

01:37:08.580 --> 01:37:10.760
And look what happened.

01:37:10.760 --> 01:37:14.280
This is the Kubernetes
project, 4,000 projects,

01:37:14.280 --> 01:37:18.710
400 years of effort went
into Kubernetes, 400 years

01:37:18.710 --> 01:37:20.980
in the last 3 years.

01:37:20.980 --> 01:37:24.460
Now you think, what
does it matter?

01:37:24.460 --> 01:37:28.950
Well, at Google, we believe
that freedom matters most.

01:37:28.950 --> 01:37:31.530
You write your code
once, and then you

01:37:31.530 --> 01:37:33.960
decide, where am
I going to run it,

01:37:33.960 --> 01:37:37.005
on-premise, in my private
cloud, maybe, maybe

01:37:37.005 --> 01:37:40.140
in multiple clouds, maybe
on the Google Cloud?

01:37:40.140 --> 01:37:45.480
So in essence, it means, at
any point, you can leave.

01:37:45.480 --> 01:37:49.110
And it also means that, at
any point, you can join.

01:37:49.110 --> 01:37:52.680
Now, this is true for
the stuff that you're

01:37:52.680 --> 01:37:54.240
orchestrating with Kubernetes.

01:37:54.240 --> 01:37:56.622
It also accounts for
Kubernetes instances, itself.

01:37:56.622 --> 01:37:57.580
You can run it with us.

01:37:57.580 --> 01:37:59.580
You can also use our
managed Kubernetes, right?

01:37:59.580 --> 01:38:01.620
And it also is true for--

01:38:01.620 --> 01:38:03.270
well, take TensorFlow.

01:38:03.270 --> 01:38:04.530
We open sourced that, right?

01:38:04.530 --> 01:38:05.800
So you can run it on-premise.

01:38:05.800 --> 01:38:06.966
You can run it in the cloud.

01:38:06.966 --> 01:38:09.780
But you also can use
our managed services

01:38:09.780 --> 01:38:11.400
for machine learning, Cloud ML.

01:38:11.400 --> 01:38:14.560
Or you can even go higher
and use our trained data

01:38:14.560 --> 01:38:18.420
sets, like we saw beautiful
demos of the Video API and all

01:38:18.420 --> 01:38:22.070
that stuff from Lee.

01:38:22.070 --> 01:38:23.980
The second part I
would like to touch

01:38:23.980 --> 01:38:28.810
is how open we are for
other technologies.

01:38:28.810 --> 01:38:30.910
How many of you,
in the audience,

01:38:30.910 --> 01:38:35.230
are in financial services
and touching COBOL?

01:38:35.230 --> 01:38:36.400
Somewhere, yeah.

01:38:36.400 --> 01:38:38.230
So it happens, right?

01:38:38.230 --> 01:38:42.340
So anything that you
can run in a container,

01:38:42.340 --> 01:38:45.730
now you can point at
with App Engine Flex.

01:38:45.730 --> 01:38:47.970
And you can pull it
in and run it inside.

01:38:47.970 --> 01:38:50.860
So next to App Engine, how
we support our platforms,

01:38:50.860 --> 01:38:53.230
like a Python and Go
and all the seven,

01:38:53.230 --> 01:38:56.020
you can now also
add all your code

01:38:56.020 --> 01:38:57.930
and put it in and use
App Engine Flex, which

01:38:57.930 --> 01:39:00.250
is absolutely awesome.

01:39:00.250 --> 01:39:04.540
Another piece I'm proud of
is our continuous support

01:39:04.540 --> 01:39:06.400
for Windows.

01:39:06.400 --> 01:39:10.330
And not to just be an OK
platform to run Windows code,

01:39:10.330 --> 01:39:13.480
no, we really want to be the
best, high performing place

01:39:13.480 --> 01:39:15.820
for Windows to be,
so people really

01:39:15.820 --> 01:39:19.480
like to bring their code,
bring Visual Studio.

01:39:19.480 --> 01:39:21.610
For example, Visual
Studio has an SDK

01:39:21.610 --> 01:39:26.230
to seamlessly integrate your
IDE-integrated development

01:39:26.230 --> 01:39:28.300
environment into our cloud.

01:39:28.300 --> 01:39:31.345
We support all versions of SQL
Surfer, Web Edition, Standard

01:39:31.345 --> 01:39:35.860
Edition, and we also announced
support for Enterprise Edition.

01:39:35.860 --> 01:39:41.530
We have numerous support
for pre-installed images

01:39:41.530 --> 01:39:43.270
for Windows Server,
all those versions.

01:39:43.270 --> 01:39:48.850
And also, now and better
Windows support for .Net Core.

01:39:48.850 --> 01:39:50.380
So we touched open
source and how

01:39:50.380 --> 01:39:53.380
we believe that it's
speeding up our innovation.

01:39:53.380 --> 01:39:58.510
We touched being open for other
code and other technologies.

01:39:58.510 --> 01:40:02.560
Now, I would like to look
at pricing and flexibility

01:40:02.560 --> 01:40:04.090
of pricing.

01:40:04.090 --> 01:40:07.210
When I speak with
people about their bill,

01:40:07.210 --> 01:40:11.570
their spend on the cloud,
they're usually not that happy.

01:40:11.570 --> 01:40:14.800
And that is not because of cost.

01:40:14.800 --> 01:40:16.540
Usually that is
because of waste.

01:40:19.530 --> 01:40:23.500
That is fixed VMs,
per hour billing,

01:40:23.500 --> 01:40:27.070
and then having three-year
leases off those fixed VMs.

01:40:27.070 --> 01:40:29.260
And I got told,
somewhere, that cloud

01:40:29.260 --> 01:40:34.090
is about flexible resource
and elastic resource

01:40:34.090 --> 01:40:36.010
capacity and all that stuff.

01:40:36.010 --> 01:40:40.240
So Google believes that
pricing should be flexible,

01:40:40.240 --> 01:40:43.990
and that you should
have minimum waste.

01:40:43.990 --> 01:40:50.700
And in March, it was, in
2014, Google committed itself,

01:40:50.700 --> 01:40:53.295
ourself, to follow Moore's law.

01:40:53.295 --> 01:40:55.420
You remember, every year
doubling of compute power.

01:40:55.420 --> 01:40:57.190
So where does the margin go?

01:40:57.190 --> 01:40:59.810
In the 6%, but the
cloud was going down.

01:40:59.810 --> 01:41:01.980
And then we started
driving the price down.

01:41:01.980 --> 01:41:03.550
Stick with Moore's law.

01:41:03.550 --> 01:41:05.170
And we started innovating.

01:41:05.170 --> 01:41:09.810
An example of this innovation
is automatic, sustained, usage

01:41:09.810 --> 01:41:10.330
discount.

01:41:10.330 --> 01:41:12.980
And honestly, you
cannot screw this up.

01:41:12.980 --> 01:41:18.070
Really, you run a VM, and after
the first quarter of the month,

01:41:18.070 --> 01:41:20.980
discount starts kicking
in, automatically,

01:41:20.980 --> 01:41:23.219
until 30% in the
end of the month.

01:41:23.219 --> 01:41:24.010
It's pretty unique.

01:41:26.740 --> 01:41:31.660
Another example is how we
have custom machine types.

01:41:31.660 --> 01:41:35.900
If you have a 24-core,
60 gigabyte RAM instance,

01:41:35.900 --> 01:41:38.230
if you need that,
we believe that you

01:41:38.230 --> 01:41:40.150
should be able to run that.

01:41:40.150 --> 01:41:45.310
And this reminds me, vividly,
of after all the work

01:41:45.310 --> 01:41:47.560
on technology and vision
with Spotify that we did--

01:41:47.560 --> 01:41:49.190
I know them very well--

01:41:49.190 --> 01:41:50.980
we had commercial negotiations.

01:41:50.980 --> 01:41:55.420
And they had clusters,
thousands of nodes for Hadoop,

01:41:55.420 --> 01:41:57.460
and they had exotic instances.

01:41:57.460 --> 01:41:59.200
And we were trying
to create a business

01:41:59.200 --> 01:42:02.860
case, because the strategy
was, we first lift then shift,

01:42:02.860 --> 01:42:05.770
and then we peel off
that Hadoop cluster

01:42:05.770 --> 01:42:07.630
and get on the
higher level servers,

01:42:07.630 --> 01:42:10.480
like Sebastien said
in the beginning.

01:42:10.480 --> 01:42:13.500
And we could not close
the business case,

01:42:13.500 --> 01:42:17.380
either too much CPU or
cores or too much memory.

01:42:17.380 --> 01:42:19.521
And then we did
something special.

01:42:19.521 --> 01:42:20.520
Tommy Kircher was there.

01:42:20.520 --> 01:42:22.770
He said, hey, let me call
some folks in the dungeon,

01:42:22.770 --> 01:42:25.360
and we create a special queue
for them, a special instance

01:42:25.360 --> 01:42:26.020
type.

01:42:26.020 --> 01:42:27.280
And we did.

01:42:27.280 --> 01:42:30.700
And there we could
compare apples to apples.

01:42:30.700 --> 01:42:33.280
And now, we have
available, for you,

01:42:33.280 --> 01:42:35.890
custom machine types, so
you can compare apples

01:42:35.890 --> 01:42:37.750
to apples in your environment.

01:42:41.190 --> 01:42:43.767
And if you don't know
what the right size is,

01:42:43.767 --> 01:42:45.350
we are monitoring
your infrastructure.

01:42:45.350 --> 01:42:47.590
So we will recommend you.

01:42:47.590 --> 01:42:49.760
We won't do music
recommendations to you.

01:42:49.760 --> 01:42:52.370
But this is more like a
machine-type recommendation.

01:42:52.370 --> 01:42:54.110
So you will get it right.

01:42:54.110 --> 01:42:56.900
And usually, in the beginning,
through development,

01:42:56.900 --> 01:42:58.550
your requirements
from your environment

01:42:58.550 --> 01:43:02.150
is very, very different than
in the times of your operations

01:43:02.150 --> 01:43:03.060
later on.

01:43:03.060 --> 01:43:04.010
So things change.

01:43:04.010 --> 01:43:06.230
So if you are planning
your infrastructure

01:43:06.230 --> 01:43:09.290
in development phase, you
might go off of the line.

01:43:09.290 --> 01:43:12.470
